{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b1e6e314e4441623", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tMusicLayout: Explicit Structural Planning\n2\tfor Controllable Text-to-Music Generation\n3\tShuyu Li1\n4\t\n5\tKejun Zhang1,3∗\n6\tJiahe Lei4\n7\tShulei Ji2,3\n8\tJiaxing Yu1\n9\tWanying Wu6\n10\tLei Wang7\n11\t\n12\tZihao Wang2,5\n13\t\n14\t1\n15\t\n16\tarXiv:2608.09035v1 [cs.SD] 10 Aug 2026\n17\t\n18\t2\n19\t\n20\tCollege of Artificial Intelligence, Zhejiang University\n21\tCollege of Computer Science and Technology, Zhejiang University\n22\t3\n23\tInnovation Center of Yangtze River Delta, Zhejiang University\n24\t4\n25\tThe Chinese University of Hong Kong\n26\t5\n27\tShandong University\n28\t6\n29\tChu Kochen Honors College, Zhejiang University\n30\t7\n31\tAnt Group\n32\t\n33\t{lsyxary, zhangkejun, shuleiji, yujx, 3240100265}@zju.edu.cn\n34\t1155261729@ee.cuhk.edu.hk, carlwang1212@gmail.com, thirtyking@163.com\n35\t\n36\tAbstract\n37\tText-to-music generation has advanced rapidly, but current systems still rely primarily on\n38\tglobal text prompts, leaving the structural organization of generated music implicit and difficult\n39\tto inspect, control, or revise before audio generation. To address this issue, we introduce\n40\tMusicLayout, an explicit intermediate representation for controlling musical structure in text-tomusic generation. MusicLayout describes a musical piece as a time-aligned layout of sections,\n41\ttextures, repetitions, variations, and instrument-level arrangements, serving as an interpretable\n42\tplanning layer between textual intent and the generated music. We integrate MusicLayout into\n43\ta text-to-music framework built on a unified autoregressive formulation, where the model first\n44\tgenerates a MusicLayout representation and subsequently predicts audio tokens conditioned on\n45\tthis representation within a single sequence. The resulting MusicLayout can be inspected and\n46\tmodified prior to audio generation, providing a mechanism for layout-level structural control. We\n47\tevaluate MusicLayout through layout-conditioned generation, layout manipulation experiments,\n48\tand matched-data ablations, providing evidence that explicit layout planning can improve\n49\tlong-range structural organization and support layout-level control.\n50\t\n51\t1\n52\t\n53\tIntroduction\n54\t\n55\tRecent text-to-music models have advanced substantially in fidelity, semantic alignment, musicality,\n56\tand efficiency [19]. They typically generate discrete audio tokens autoregressively or synthesize\n57\tcontinuous and compressed representations with diffusion models [1, 4, 20]. Recent systems further\n58\tsupport long-form generation and combine language-model (LM) reasoning with diffusion-based\n59\tacoustic synthesis [10, 13].\n60\tDespite these advances, most systems rely on global text prompts and leave musical planning\n61\timplicit. A prompt can describe genre, mood, instrumentation, or tempo, but cannot precisely\n62\t∗\n63\t\n64\tCorresponding author.\n65\t\n66\t1\n67\t\n68\t\fLM-based Text-to-audio generation\n69\tText Prompt\n70\t\n71\taudio codes\n72\t\n73\t❄ Audio-code\n74\tDecoder\n75\t\n76\tLarge Language Audio Model\n77\t\n78\tMusic wave\n79\tA mid-tempo Chinese\n80\tpop folk arrangement\n81\twith guitar, accordion,\n82\tpiano, and simple\n83\tpercussion …..\n84\t\n85\tLM-based Text-to-audio generation with a planning stage\n86\tLayout Tokens\n87\t\n88\tText Prompt\n89\t<layout>\n90\t\n91\taudio codes\n92\t</layout >\n93\t\n94\t🔥 Large Language Audio Model\n95\tPlan Stage\n96\t\n97\t<layout >\n98\t\n99\t</layout >\n100\t\n101\tRewrite plan: time=49-55 label=chorus <roles> drums\n102\t\n103\tFigure 1: Conventional autoregressive LM-based text-to-audio generation (top) and our explicitplanning framework (bottom). Our LM generates MusicLayout tokens and then discrete audio\n104\ttokens, labeled audio codes in the figure, within one sequence. A frozen decoder renders the audio\n105\ttokens into music. The abbreviated rewrite selects the musical section at 49–55 seconds, assigns it\n106\tthe chorus label, and sets its active instrument list to drums. The <roles> tag delimits that list.\n107\tspecify how sections are organized, materials recur or vary, textures evolve, or instruments enter and\n108\tleave. Existing controllable methods add melody, chords, drums, dynamics, symbolic lead sheets, or\n109\tsemantic representations [2, 13, 26, 34, 39]. These controls are often limited to individual attributes,\n110\trequire additional musical inputs, or remain implicit. They do not expose a unified, time-aligned\n111\trepresentation for specifying the organization and arrangement of an entire piece.\n112\tExplicit planning representations improve controllability and interpretability in image generation\n113\tby exposing high-level organization before content. PlanGen [14], for example, generates spatial\n114\tlayout tokens before image tokens in one autoregressive sequence. For music, this principle could\n115\timprove long-range structural organization and controllability. Transferring it requires representing\n116\torganization along time rather than space.\n117\tFocusing on instrumental music, we introduce MusicLayout, an explicit intermediate representation for planning musical structure in audio language models. It describes section transitions,\n118\trepetitions and variations, texture changes, and instrument participation along a shared timeline.\n119\tAs illustrated in Figure 1, given a text prompt, our model generates a MusicLayout representation\n120\tand subsequently predicts audio tokens conditioned on it within a single sequence. This makes\n121\tstructural planning inspectable before synthesis and supplies audio generation with an explicit\n122\tdescription of how the music should unfold. Experiments on layout conditioning and manipulation\n123\tprovide evidence that MusicLayout can improve structural organization and support layout-level\n124\tcontrol.\n125\tOur main contributions are summarized as follows:\n126\t• We propose MusicLayout, an explicit planning representation for unified audio language\n127\t2\n128\t\n129\t\fmodels. It represents music as a time-aligned layout of sections, repetitions, variations,\n130\ttextures, and instrument arrangements, providing structured musical plans beyond global\n131\ttextual prompts.\n132\t• We develop a unified autoregressive framework for layout planning and audio generation,\n133\twhere a single model first produces an explicit MusicLayout plan and then continues to\n134\tgenerate audio tokens conditioned on the preceding layout within a single sequence. By\n135\tmaking musical planning explicit, this framework supports long-range structural organization\n136\tand layout-level control.\n137\t• Through evaluations of layout-conditioned generation, layout manipulation, and\n138\tmatched-data ablations, we provide evidence that MusicLayout can serve as an interpretable\n139\tplanning interface and improve long-range structural organization in text-to-music generation.\n140\t\n141\t2\n142\t\n143\tRelated Work\n144\t\n145\t2.1\n146\t\n147\tText-to-Music Generation\n148\t\n149\tAutoregressive music models predict discrete audio tokens, as exemplified by Jukebox, MusicLM,\n150\tand MusicGen [1, 4, 6]. Diffusion-based systems instead synthesize waveforms, spectrograms, or\n151\tcompressed latents through iterative denoising [16, 23, 31]. AudioLDM 2 and MeLoDy combine\n152\tLM-based semantic modeling with diffusion synthesis [18, 20]. Stable Audio 3 supports efficient\n153\tvariable-length generation, while ACE-Step 1.5 uses a hybrid architecture in which an LM performs\n154\thigh-level planning and a Diffusion Transformer (DiT) realizes the audio [10, 13, 28]. Although some\n155\tsystems incorporate high-level planning, temporal organization and arrangement remain implicit or\n156\tcoarsely represented.\n157\t\n158\t2.2\n159\t\n160\tControllable Text-to-Music Generation\n161\t\n162\tPrior systems supplement global prompts with melody references, rhythm, dynamics, chords, drums,\n163\tor other symbolic and audio conditions [1, 4, 26, 34, 39]. Seed-Music uses a symbolic lead-sheet\n164\tpipeline [2]. ACE-Step 1.5 uses metadata and song-blueprint planning, but its blueprint remains\n165\timplicit in the learned audio-token representations and is not exposed as an interpretable structural\n166\tplan [13]. Other methods use individual controls, additional inputs, task-specific modules, or coarse\n167\tmetadata. MusicLayout instead exposes sections, textures, repetitions, variations, and instrument\n168\tarrangements in an inspectable and adjustable time-aligned plan.\n169\t\n170\t3\n171\t\n172\tMethodology\n173\t\n174\tWe build our model upon ACE-Step 1.5, extending its LM to generate a MusicLayout before\n175\tproducing layout-conditioned audio tokens. The original audio tokenizer and DiT remain frozen,\n176\twith the latter rendering the tokens into a waveform.\n177\t\n178\t3.1\n179\t\n180\tMusicLayout Representation\n181\t\n182\tMusicLayout externalizes temporal musical organization as a structured, human-readable sequence\n183\tthat bridges high-level structural intent and audio-token generation. It follows three design principles:\n184\ttemporal alignment, hierarchical organization, and controllability.\n185\t\n186\t3\n187\t\n188\t\f0\n189\t\n190\t8\n191\t\n192\t16\n193\t\n194\t50\n195\t\n196\t64\n197\t\n198\t98\n199\t\n200\t110\n201\t\n202\t144\n203\t\n204\t152\n205\t\n206\t160\n207\t\n208\t172\n209\t\n210\t190\n211\t\n212\tTime (s)\n213\tSections\n214\t\n215\tintro\n216\t\n217\tchorus\n218\t\n219\tverse\n220\t\n221\tchorus\n222\t\n223\tverse\n224\t\n225\tchorus\n226\t\n227\tbridge\n228\t\n229\tchorus chorus\n230\t\n231\tchorus\n232\t\n233\toutro\n234\t\n235\tFamily\n236\t\n237\tfam_0 fam_0\n238\t\n239\tfam_1\n240\t\n241\tfam_0\n242\t\n243\tfam_1\n244\t\n245\tfam_0\n246\t\n247\tfam_2\n248\t\n249\tfam_0 fam_0\n250\t\n251\tfam_0\n252\t\n253\tfam_3\n254\t\n255\tTextures\n256\t\n257\trhythm rhythm\n258\t\n259\tharmonic_bed\n260\t\n261\trhythm\n262\t\n263\tharmonic_bed\n264\t\n265\trhythm\n266\t\n267\tbuild_up\n268\t\n269\trhythm rhythm\n270\t\n271\trhythm\n272\t\n273\tother\n274\t\n275\tVariation\n276\t\n277\tsame\n278\t\n279\tstrong\n280\t\n281\tsame\n282\t\n283\tlight\n284\t\n285\tsame\n286\t\n287\tlight\n288\t\n289\tunique\n290\t\n291\tlight\n292\t\n293\tlight\n294\t\n295\tunique\n296\t\n297\tlow /\n298\tsparse /\n299\tmed\n300\t\n301\tlow /\n302\tmed /\n303\tmed\n304\t\n305\tlow /\n306\tmed /\n307\tmed\n308\t\n309\tlow /\n310\tmed /\n311\tmed\n312\t\n313\tlow /\n314\tmed /\n315\tmed\n316\t\n317\tlow /\n318\tmed /\n319\tmed\n320\t\n321\tlow /\n322\tmed /\n323\tmed\n324\t\n325\tlow /\n326\tmed /\n327\tmed\n328\t\n329\tlow /\n330\tmed /\n331\tmed\n332\t\n333\tlow /\n334\tmed /\n335\tmed\n336\t\n337\tlow /\n338\tmed /\n339\tmed\n340\t\n341\tlow /\n342\tmed /\n343\tmed\n344\t\n345\tlow /\n346\tmed /\n347\tmed\n348\t\n349\tlow /\n350\tmed /\n351\tmed\n352\t\n353\tmid /\n354\tmed /\n355\tmed\n356\t\n357\tmid /\n358\tsparse /\n359\tmed\n360\t\n361\tmid /\n362\tmed /\n363\tmed\n364\t\n365\tlight\n366\t\n367\tInstruments\n368\t(Arrangement)\n369\t\n370\t🥁 drums\n371\tbass\n372\t\n373\t🎷 sax\n374\t\n375\tlow /\n376\tmed /\n377\tmed\n378\tmid /\n379\tmed /\n380\tmed\n381\t\n382\tLegend:\n383\t\n384\tmid /\n385\tmed /\n386\tmed\n387\t\n388\tfam_0\n389\t\n390\tfam_1\n391\t\n392\tfam_2\n393\t\n394\tfam_3\n395\t\n396\tlow /\n397\tmed /\n398\tmed\n399\t\n400\tSame family color = recurring musical material. Role = register / density / energy; empty = inactive.\n401\t\n402\tFigure 2: A MusicLayout example aligned to a shared timeline. Rows show sections, material\n403\tfamilies, textures, variations, and instrument arrangements. Family colors identify recurring material,\n404\tand instrument entries give register, density, and energy. Blanks denote inactivity. For readability,\n405\trhythm, light, and strong abbreviate rhythm_driven, light_var, and strong_var, respectively.\n406\tA MusicLayout combines piece-level structural relationships among musical materials with\n407\ttime-aligned segments, each corresponding to one musical section. At the piece level, MusicLayout\n408\torganizes recurring musical materials and their relationships, including repetitions and variations\n409\tacross sections. At the segment level, each segment is associated with a time-aligned span and\n410\tdescribes its section identity, texture evolution, and instrument participation. Its functional section\n411\tlabel is selected from intro, verse, prechorus, chorus, bridge, breakdown, outro, transition,\n412\thook, solo, and build. Figure 2 illustrates these attributes along a shared timeline. MusicLayout\n413\tis serialized as discrete tokens, allowing it to be directly modeled by an autoregressive LM.\n414\tA family is a piece-local group of segments that share similar musical material. Labels such\n415\tas fam_0 and fam_1 identify families only within the current piece. The same label may denote\n416\tdifferent musical material in another piece. Each segment is marked as unique, same, light_var,\n417\tor strong_var. These values indicate, respectively, that the segment has no repeated family, closely\n418\trepeats its family pattern, or departs from that pattern to a smaller or larger degree. Thus, the\n419\tfamily colors and the variation row in Figure 2 jointly describe which material returns and how\n420\tmuch it changes.\n421\tThe texture field gives a coarse description of how a musical segment is organized. Table 1 defines\n422\tits vocabulary. Each segment contains a <roles> block listing its active instruments. Figures 1\n423\tand 2 use the form instrument:register:density:energy, for example drums:low:sparse:med.\n424\tThe instrument field uses a predefined vocabulary of 25 categories. Each instrument is followed\n425\tby register (low/mid/high), density (sparse/med/dense), and energy (low/med/high), encoding\n426\tpitch range, activity, and intensity, respectively. Figure 1 abbreviates the edited field as drums.\n427\tWe derived MusicLayout annotations from symbolic representations by extracting temporal\n428\tsections, repetitions, variations, textures, and instrument arrangements. We rendered the symbolic\n429\tmusic into audio and aligned each layout with its audio and text prompt to form prompt–layout–audio\n430\ttraining triples.\n431\t\n432\t4\n433\t\n434\t\fCategory\n435\t\n436\tMeaning\n437\t\n438\tlayered\n439\trhythm_driven\n440\tmelodic_front\n441\tharmonic_bed\n442\tpercussive\n443\tbuild_up\n444\tsparse_pulse\n445\tlead_front\n446\tcontrast\n447\tother\n448\t\n449\tCoexisting lead and supporting layers\n450\tRhythm-section-centered arrangement\n451\tForegrounded melodic material\n452\tSustained harmonic support\n453\tPercussion-centered material\n454\tAccumulating or intensifying layers\n455\tSparse intermittent activity\n456\tForegrounded lead material\n457\tDeliberately contrasting texture\n458\tCases outside the listed categories\n459\t\n460\tTable 1: Texture categories in MusicLayout.\n461\t\n462\t3.2\n463\t\n464\tUnified Layout Planning and Audio Generation\n465\t\n466\tModeling. Given conditioning context c, the LM serializes MusicLayout sequence l before audiotoken sequence a within a single autoregressive sequence:\n467\ty = [c, <layout>, l, </layout>, a].\n468\t\n469\t(1)\n470\t\n471\twhere <layout> and </layout> delimit the planning region. This ordering makes layout generation\n472\tprecede and condition audio-token generation. The corresponding joint distribution factorizes as\n473\tp(l, a | c) = p(l | c)p(a | c, l).\n474\t\n475\t(2)\n476\t\n477\tHere, p(l | c) describes layout planning from the conditioning context, while p(a | c, l) describes\n478\taudio-token generation given the context and layout.\n479\tTraining. We train the LM on two complementary next-token tasks. Layout planning trains\n480\tthe model to generate l from c. Layout-conditioned audio generation trains it to predict a from c\n481\tand a ground-truth (GT) MusicLayout:\n482\tLplan = − t log p(lt | c, l<t ),\n483\tP\n484\tLaudio = − i log p(ai | c, l, a<i ).\n485\tP\n486\t\n487\t(3)\n488\t\n489\tWe initially optimize layout planning to learn the MusicLayout schema and the schema-specific\n490\tspecial tokens used to mark its fields and boundaries. We then alternate the tasks, applying the loss\n491\tonly to the corresponding layout or audio target span. Only the LM is updated. The pretrained\n492\taudio tokenizer and DiT synthesis components remain frozen.\n493\tInference and control. In automatic inference, the LM generates a MusicLayout from the\n494\tconditioning context and then continues with audio tokens. Alternatively, the human-readable\n495\tlayout can be inspected and adjusted before audio generation to change temporal organization or\n496\tinstrument arrangement. The revised layout and original context form the autoregressive prefix for\n497\taudio-token generation, providing a pre-synthesis interface for layout-level control.\n498\t\n499\t4\n500\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "b6358724-e349-47d8-9c24-ec80d9e01c36", "uuid": "c2d02ddf-3086-4238-8bfd-7651111a6f35", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T18:30:44.992Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/2608.09035v1.txt", "content": "MusicLayout: Explicit Structural Planning\nfor Controllable Text-to-Music Generation\nShuyu Li1\n\nKejun Zhang1,3∗\nJiahe Lei4\nShulei Ji2,3\nJiaxing Yu1\nWanying Wu6\nLei Wang7\n\nZihao Wang2,5\n\n1\n\narXiv:2608.09035v1 [cs.SD] 10 Aug 2026\n\n2\n\nCollege of Artificial Intelligence, Zhejiang University\nCollege of Computer Science and Technology, Zhejiang University\n3\nInnovation Center of Yangtze River Delta, Zhejiang University\n4\nThe Chinese University of Hong Kong\n5\nShandong University\n6\nChu Kochen Honors College, Zhejiang University\n7\nAnt Group\n\n{lsyxary, zhangkejun, shuleiji, yujx, 3240100265}@zju.edu.cn\n1155261729@ee.cuhk.edu.hk, carlwang1212@gmail.com, thirtyking@163.com\n\nAbstract\nText-to-music generation has advanced rapidly, but current systems still rely primarily on\nglobal text prompts, leaving the structural organization of generated music implicit and difficult\nto inspect, control, or revise before audio generation. To address this issue, we introduce\nMusicLayout, an explicit intermediate representation for controlling musical structure in text-tomusic generation. MusicLayout describes a musical piece as a time-aligned layout of sections,\ntextures, repetitions, variations, and instrument-level arrangements, serving as an interpretable\nplanning layer between textual intent and the generated music. We integrate MusicLayout into\na text-to-music framework built on a unified autoregressive formulation, where the model first\ngenerates a MusicLayout representation and subsequently predicts audio tokens conditioned on\nthis representation within a single sequence. The resulting MusicLayout can be inspected and\nmodified prior to audio generation, providing a mechanism for layout-level structural control. We\nevaluate MusicLayout through layout-conditioned generation, layout manipulation experiments,\nand matched-data ablations, providing evidence that explicit layout planning can improve\nlong-range structural organization and support layout-level control.\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nRecent text-to-music models have advanced substantially in fidelity, semantic alignment, musicality,\nand efficiency [19]. They typically generate discrete audio tokens autoregressively or synthesize\ncontinuous and compressed representations with diffusion models [1, 4, 20]. Recent systems further\nsupport long-form generation and combine language-model (LM) reasoning with diffusion-based\nacoustic synthesis [10, 13].\nDespite these advances, most systems rely on global text prompts and leave musical planning\nimplicit. A prompt can describe genre, mood, instrumentation, or tempo, but cannot precisely\n∗\n\nCorresponding author.\n\n1\n\n\fLM-based Text-to-audio generation\nText Prompt\n\naudio codes\n\n❄ Audio-code\nDecoder\n\nLarge Language Audio Model\n\nMusic wave\nA mid-tempo Chinese\npop folk arrangement\nwith guitar, accordion,\npiano, and simple\npercussion …..\n\nLM-based Text-to-audio generation with a planning stage\nLayout Tokens\n\nText Prompt\n<layout>\n\naudio codes\n</layout >\n\n🔥 Large Language Audio Model\nPlan Stage\n\n<layout >\n\n</layout >\n\nRewrite plan: time=49-55 label=chorus <roles> drums\n\nFigure 1: Conventional autoregressive LM-based text-to-audio generation (top) and our explicitplanning framework (bottom). Our LM generates MusicLayout tokens and then discrete audio\ntokens, labeled audio codes in the figure, within one sequence. A frozen decoder renders the audio\ntokens into music. The abbreviated rewrite selects the musical section at 49–55 seconds, assigns it\nthe chorus label, and sets its active instrument list to drums. The <roles> tag delimits that list.\nspecify how sections are organized, materials recur or vary, textures evolve, or instruments enter and\nleave. Existing controllable methods add melody, chords, drums, dynamics, symbolic lead sheets, or\nsemantic representations [2, 13, 26, 34, 39]. These controls are often limited to individual attributes,\nrequire additional musical inputs, or remain implicit. They do not expose a unified, time-aligned\nrepresentation for specifying the organization and arrangement of an entire piece.\nExplicit planning representations improve controllability and interpretability in image generation\nby exposing high-level organization before content. PlanGen [14], for example, generates spatial\nlayout tokens before image tokens in one autoregressive sequence. For music, this principle could\nimprove long-range structural organization and controllability. Transferring it requires representing\norganization along time rather than space.\nFocusing on instrumental music, we introduce MusicLayout, an explicit intermediate representation for planning musical structure in audio language models. It describes section transitions,\nrepetitions and variations, texture changes, and instrument participation along a shared timeline.\nAs illustrated in Figure 1, given a text prompt, our model generates a MusicLayout representation\nand subsequently predicts audio tokens conditioned on it within a single sequence. This makes\nstructural planning inspectable before synthesis and supplies audio generation with an explicit\ndescription of how the music should unfold. Experiments on layout conditioning and manipulation\nprovide evidence that MusicLayout can improve structural organization and support layout-level\ncontrol.\nOur main contributions are summarized as follows:\n• We propose MusicLayout, an explicit planning representation for unified audio language\n2\n\n\fmodels. It represents music as a time-aligned layout of sections, repetitions, variations,\ntextures, and instrument arrangements, providing structured musical plans beyond global\ntextual prompts.\n• We develop a unified autoregressive framework for layout planning and audio generation,\nwhere a single model first produces an explicit MusicLayout plan and then continues to\ngenerate audio tokens conditioned on the preceding layout within a single sequence. By\nmaking musical planning explicit, this framework supports long-range structural organization\nand layout-level control.\n• Through evaluations of layout-conditioned generation, layout manipulation, and\nmatched-data ablations, we provide evidence that MusicLayout can serve as an interpretable\nplanning interface and improve long-range structural organization in text-to-music generation.\n\n2\n\nRelated Work\n\n2.1\n\nText-to-Music Generation\n\nAutoregressive music models predict discrete audio tokens, as exemplified by Jukebox, MusicLM,\nand MusicGen [1, 4, 6]. Diffusion-based systems instead synthesize waveforms, spectrograms, or\ncompressed latents through iterative denoising [16, 23, 31]. AudioLDM 2 and MeLoDy combine\nLM-based semantic modeling with diffusion synthesis [18, 20]. Stable Audio 3 supports efficient\nvariable-length generation, while ACE-Step 1.5 uses a hybrid architecture in which an LM performs\nhigh-level planning and a Diffusion Transformer (DiT) realizes the audio [10, 13, 28]. Although some\nsystems incorporate high-level planning, temporal organization and arrangement remain implicit or\ncoarsely represented.\n\n2.2\n\nControllable Text-to-Music Generation\n\nPrior systems supplement global prompts with melody references, rhythm, dynamics, chords, drums,\nor other symbolic and audio conditions [1, 4, 26, 34, 39]. Seed-Music uses a symbolic lead-sheet\npipeline [2]. ACE-Step 1.5 uses metadata and song-blueprint planning, but its blueprint remains\nimplicit in the learned audio-token representations and is not exposed as an interpretable structural\nplan [13]. Other methods use individual controls, additional inputs, task-specific modules, or coarse\nmetadata. MusicLayout instead exposes sections, textures, repetitions, variations, and instrument\narrangements in an inspectable and adjustable time-aligned plan.\n\n3\n\nMethodology\n\nWe build our model upon ACE-Step 1.5, extending its LM to generate a MusicLayout before\nproducing layout-conditioned audio tokens. The original audio tokenizer and DiT remain frozen,\nwith the latter rendering the tokens into a waveform.\n\n3.1\n\nMusicLayout Representation\n\nMusicLayout externalizes temporal musical organization as a structured, human-readable sequence\nthat bridges high-level structural intent and audio-token generation. It follows three design principles:\ntemporal alignment, hierarchical organization, and controllability.\n\n3\n\n\f0\n\n8\n\n16\n\n50\n\n64\n\n98\n\n110\n\n144\n\n152\n\n160\n\n172\n\n190\n\nTime (s)\nSections\n\nintro\n\nchorus\n\nverse\n\nchorus\n\nverse\n\nchorus\n\nbridge\n\nchorus chorus\n\nchorus\n\noutro\n\nFamily\n\nfam_0 fam_0\n\nfam_1\n\nfam_0\n\nfam_1\n\nfam_0\n\nfam_2\n\nfam_0 fam_0\n\nfam_0\n\nfam_3\n\nTextures\n\nrhythm rhythm\n\nharmonic_bed\n\nrhythm\n\nharmonic_bed\n\nrhythm\n\nbuild_up\n\nrhythm rhythm\n\nrhythm\n\nother\n\nVariation\n\nsame\n\nstrong\n\nsame\n\nlight\n\nsame\n\nlight\n\nunique\n\nlight\n\nlight\n\nunique\n\nlow /\nsparse /\nmed\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\n\nmid /\nmed /\nmed\n\nmid /\nsparse /\nmed\n\nmid /\nmed /\nmed\n\nlight\n\nInstruments\n(Arrangement)\n\n🥁 drums\nbass\n\n🎷 sax\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\nmid /\nmed /\nmed\n\nLegend:\n\nmid /\nmed /\nmed\n\nfam_0\n\nfam_1\n\nfam_2\n\nfam_3\n\nlow /\nmed /\nmed\n\nSame family color = recurring musical material. Role = register / density / energy; empty = inactive.\n\nFigure 2: A MusicLayout example aligned to a shared timeline. Rows show sections, material\nfamilies, textures, variations, and instrument arrangements. Family colors identify recurring material,\nand instrument entries give register, density, and energy. Blanks denote inactivity. For readability,\nrhythm, light, and strong abbreviate rhythm_driven, light_var, and strong_var, respectively.\nA MusicLayout combines piece-level structural relationships among musical materials with\ntime-aligned segments, each corresponding to one musical section. At the piece level, MusicLayout\norganizes recurring musical materials and their relationships, including repetitions and variations\nacross sections. At the segment level, each segment is associated with a time-aligned span and\ndescribes its section identity, texture evolution, and instrument participation. Its functional section\nlabel is selected from intro, verse, prechorus, chorus, bridge, breakdown, outro, transition,\nhook, solo, and build. Figure 2 illustrates these attributes along a shared timeline. MusicLayout\nis serialized as discrete tokens, allowing it to be directly modeled by an autoregressive LM.\nA family is a piece-local group of segments that share similar musical material. Labels such\nas fam_0 and fam_1 identify families only within the current piece. The same label may denote\ndifferent musical material in another piece. Each segment is marked as unique, same, light_var,\nor strong_var. These values indicate, respectively, that the segment has no repeated family, closely\nrepeats its family pattern, or departs from that pattern to a smaller or larger degree. Thus, the\nfamily colors and the variation row in Figure 2 jointly describe which material returns and how\nmuch it changes.\nThe texture field gives a coarse description of how a musical segment is organized. Table 1 defines\nits vocabulary. Each segment contains a <roles> block listing its active instruments. Figures 1\nand 2 use the form instrument:register:density:energy, for example drums:low:sparse:med.\nThe instrument field uses a predefined vocabulary of 25 categories. Each instrument is followed\nby register (low/mid/high), density (sparse/med/dense), and energy (low/med/high), encoding\npitch range, activity, and intensity, respectively. Figure 1 abbreviates the edited field as drums.\nWe derived MusicLayout annotations from symbolic representations by extracting temporal\nsections, repetitions, variations, textures, and instrument arrangements. We rendered the symbolic\nmusic into audio and aligned each layout with its audio and text prompt to form prompt–layout–audio\ntraining triples.\n\n4\n\n\fCategory\n\nMeaning\n\nlayered\nrhythm_driven\nmelodic_front\nharmonic_bed\npercussive\nbuild_up\nsparse_pulse\nlead_front\ncontrast\nother\n\nCoexisting lead and supporting layers\nRhythm-section-centered arrangement\nForegrounded melodic material\nSustained harmonic support\nPercussion-centered material\nAccumulating or intensifying layers\nSparse intermittent activity\nForegrounded lead material\nDeliberately contrasting texture\nCases outside the listed categories\n\nTable 1: Texture categories in MusicLayout.\n\n3.2\n\nUnified Layout Planning and Audio Generation\n\nModeling. Given conditioning context c, the LM serializes MusicLayout sequence l before audiotoken sequence a within a single autoregressive sequence:\ny = [c, <layout>, l, </layout>, a].\n\n(1)\n\nwhere <layout> and </layout> delimit the planning region. This ordering makes layout generation\nprecede and condition audio-token generation. The corresponding joint distribution factorizes as\np(l, a | c) = p(l | c)p(a | c, l).\n\n(2)\n\nHere, p(l | c) describes layout planning from the conditioning context, while p(a | c, l) describes\naudio-token generation given the context and layout.\nTraining. We train the LM on two complementary next-token tasks. Layout planning trains\nthe model to generate l from c. Layout-conditioned audio generation trains it to predict a from c\nand a ground-truth (GT) MusicLayout:\nLplan = − t log p(lt | c, l<t ),\nP\nLaudio = − i log p(ai | c, l, a<i ).\nP\n\n(3)\n\nWe initially optimize layout planning to learn the MusicLayout schema and the schema-specific\nspecial tokens used to mark its fields and boundaries. We then alternate the tasks, applying the loss\nonly to the corresponding layout or audio target span. Only the LM is updated. The pretrained\naudio tokenizer and DiT synthesis components remain frozen.\nInference and control. In automatic inference, the LM generates a MusicLayout from the\nconditioning context and then continues with audio tokens. Alternatively, the human-readable\nlayout can be inspected and adjusted before audio generation to change temporal organization or\ninstrument arrangement. The revised layout and original context form the autoregressive prefix for\naudio-token generation, providing a pre-synthesis interface for layout-level control.\n\n4\n", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 2074}}}
