{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b3bb078308b32161", "type": "tool_result", "content": "500\t\n501\tExperiments\n502\t\n503\tWe evaluate generation, structural control, layout manipulation, ablations, and subjective quality\n504\tagainst representative text-to-music systems. For our framework, we consider two layout conditions.\n505\tIn the reference-layout condition, the model receives a MusicLayout derived from the target music.\n506\t5\n507\t\n508\t\fDataset\n509\t\n510\tAudio source\n511\t\n512\tTrain Eval.\n513\t\n514\tFreeMIDI MIDI-synth.\n515\t24,474 2,719\n516\tMidiCaps MIDI-synth.\n517\t– 1,040\n518\tMuChin Real, separated accomp.\n519\t– 1,000\n520\t\n521\tTable 2: Datasets used in our experiments.\n522\tThis condition jointly tests whether MusicLayout captures the target’s musical organization and\n523\twhether the model can use it to control the structure of the generated audio. In the generated-layout\n524\tcondition, the model predicts a layout from the text prompt before generating audio, evaluating the\n525\tcomplete end-to-end text-to-music process.\n526\t\n527\t4.1\n528\t\n529\tExperimental Setup\n530\t\n531\tDatasets. We used three datasets with complementary roles. FreeMIDI [12] provided the training\n532\tdata and a disjoint in-domain evaluation set. MidiCaps [24] was used to evaluate generalization to\n533\tout-of-domain MIDI, while MuChin [38] provided an out-of-domain evaluation on real audio. Their\n534\tdetails are summarized in Table 2.\n535\tFreeMIDI. We collected a subset of FreeMIDI and retained only pieces longer than 15 seconds.\n536\tFor each remaining MIDI file, we extracted a MusicLayout and synthesized its audio, forming\n537\ta layout–audio pair. We then provided the audio to MOSS-Music [27], an audio-understanding\n538\tlanguage model, to produce the generation prompt, yielding aligned prompt–layout–audio triples.\n539\tMidiCaps. MidiCaps already provides natural-language captions. We selected a subset with a\n540\tbalanced distribution of style labels and removed MIDI files that overlap with FreeMIDI. For each\n541\tremaining piece, we extracted a layout and synthesized audio from the MIDI, directly pairing both\n542\twith its caption to form evaluation triples.\n543\tMuChin. MuChin contains vocal music with Chinese captions but no MIDI, and thus cannot\n544\tsupport the reference-layout condition. We used DeepSeek-V4-Flash [40] to translate the captions\n545\tinto English and remove vocal-related descriptions. We further separated the accompaniment\n546\tfrom the vocals using a community-trained BS-RoFormer model [22] and used only the separated\n547\taccompaniment for metric computation. Generation conditions that require an externally supplied\n548\tlayout—the reference-layout and shuffled-layout-inference conditions—are not reported on MuChin.\n549\tCompared systems. We compared against MusicGen [4], ACE-Step 1.5 and ACE-Step XL-Turbo\n550\t[13], and Stable Audio 3 Medium [10]. We evaluated our model under either the generated-layout\n551\tor the reference-layout condition. To control for the effect of additional training data, we also\n552\tfinetuned ACE-Step 1.5 on the same data using the same LM adaptation setup as our model. We\n553\tdenote this matched-data no-layout control as ACE-Step 1.5-FT. It directly predicts audio from the\n554\tconditioning context c without MusicLayout tokens, serving as both the primary fair comparison\n555\tfor explicit layout planning and the first step of the progressive ablation. Systems shared evaluation\n556\titems, prompts, and seeds when supported, and baselines used officially recommended decoding\n557\tsettings. Systems without a layout representation targeted each item’s reference duration. For\n558\tlong-form MusicGen generation, we followed its official continuation mechanism. For our model,\n559\tthe generation duration is instead determined by the end time of the conditioning layout. In the\n560\tgenerated-layout condition, this duration is therefore predicted by the model rather than specified\n561\texternally, whereas the reference-layout condition matches the reference duration because its layout\n562\tis derived from the target music.\n563\t\n564\t6\n565\t\n566\t\fEvaluation protocol. We report Fréchet Audio Distance (FAD) [30] for acoustic distribution\n567\tsimilarity, PaSST-KL [17] for sound-event agreement, SSIM [37] for local time–frequency similarity,\n568\tand CLAPScore [7] for text–audio alignment.\n569\tFor long-range structure, SCM Energy Distance [5, 33] compares corpus-level structural complexity distributions, while F0.5 and F3.0 acoustic-boundary agreement [36] measure fine- and\n570\tcoarse-grained transition alignment within each piece. To examine how layout manipulation changes\n571\tlong-range organization, we also visualize recurrence-based self-similarity matrices (SSMs), following\n572\tthe structural visualization used in Stable Audio 2 and its underlying music structure analysis\n573\tmethod [9, 32].\n574\tImplementation details. We finetuned ACE-Step 1.5’s 1.7B-parameter LM using six A800\n575\tGPUs, a 4,096-token context, and an effective batch size of 48. We selected the checkpoint with the\n576\tlowest development layout-to-audio loss. Layout and audio decoding used temperature/top-p values\n577\tof 0.8/0.95 and 0.9/0.95.\n578\t\n579\t4.2\n580\t\n581\tResults\n582\t\n583\tOverall Generation Performance\n584\tTable 3 summarizes the objective results. No single system dominates all aspects of generation. Our\n585\tmodel with generated layouts records the lowest FAD and PaSST-KL on FreeMIDI. On MidiCaps, it\n586\talso records the lowest PaSST-KL, suggesting that layout-conditioned generation can remain close to\n587\tthe reference acoustic-event distribution despite out-of-domain MIDI captions. Its lower CLAPScore\n588\trelative to Stable Audio and ACE-Step XL-Turbo, however, shows that explicit planning may not\n589\tuniformly improve global text–audio correspondence.\n590\tThe matched-data no-layout control provides a more direct test of the contribution of explicit\n591\tlayout planning. It is weaker than the original ACE-Step 1.5 on nearly all metrics. This degradation\n592\tlikely reflects the training-data difference: the original model was trained on high-quality real\n593\trecordings, whereas our finetuning data consists of MIDI-synthesized audio with lower audio quality\n594\tand timbral fidelity, as well as a different acoustic distribution. Since our model and this control\n595\tshare the same finetuning data and LM adaptation setup, their comparison isolates the effect of\n596\texplicit layout planning. Relative to this control, the reference-layout condition has more favorable\n597\tvalues on all seven metrics for FreeMIDI and MidiCaps. The generated-layout condition does so\n598\ton six metrics for FreeMIDI and five for MidiCaps and MuChin. These results show that explicit\n599\tlayout planning substantially improves generation under matched-data conditions and can partly\n600\toffset the limitations of weaker training audio.\n601\tOverall, our model is competitive on the two MIDI-derived datasets, indicating that explicit\n602\tlayout planning contributes to overall music generation performance. Its FAD, PaSST-KL, and SSIM\n603\tresults on MuChin are less favorable, however. This difference likely arises from an acoustic-domain\n604\tshift: our model is trained on MIDI-synthesized instrumental audio, whereas MuChin consists of\n605\taccompaniments separated from real recordings.\n606\tStructural Control\n607\tThe reference-layout condition records the highest boundary scores shown for FreeMIDI, the highest\n608\tMidiCaps F3.0 , and a tie for the highest MidiCaps F0.5 . Because the reference layouts are derived\n609\tfrom the target music, these results provide evidence that MusicLayout captures target organization\n610\tand that conditioning on it can guide corresponding structure in the generated audio.\n611\t\n612\t7\n613\t\n614\t\fDataset\n615\t\n616\tSystem\n617\t\n618\tFAD↓\n619\t\n620\tKL↓ SSIM↑ CLAP↑ SCM↓ F0.5 ↑ F3.0 ↑\n621\t\n622\tACE-Step 1.5\n623\tACE-Step XL-Turbo\n624\tMusicGen-Large\n625\tStable Audio 3 Medium\n626\t\n627\t2.874\n628\t2.584\n629\t3.706\n630\t3.014\n631\t\n632\t0.789 0.159\n633\t0.734 0.181\n634\t0.834 0.189\n635\t0.838 0.213\n636\t\n637\tReference layout\n638\tGenerated layout\n639\t\n640\t2.610 0.740\n641\t2.495 0.719\n642\t\n643\t0.183\n644\t0.153\n645\t\n646\t0.238 0.054 0.645 0.838\n647\t0.241 0.206 0.643 0.834\n648\t\n649\tMatched-data ACE-Step 1.5-FT (no layout) 3.345 0.956\n650\tcontrols\n651\tShuffled-layout training\n652\t2.756 0.721\n653\tShuffled-layout inference\n654\t2.636 0.779\n655\t\n656\t0.154\n657\t0.153\n658\t0.176\n659\t\n660\t0.219 0.380 0.577 0.739\n661\t0.216 0.100 0.607 0.810\n662\t0.230 0.038 0.642 0.831\n663\t\n664\tGroup\n665\t\n666\tFreeMIDI Baselines\n667\t\n668\tOur model\n669\t\n670\tMidiCaps Baselines\n671\t\n672\tMuChin\n673\t\n674\t0.290\n675\t0.313\n676\t0.258\n677\t0.352\n678\t\n679\t0.117\n680\t0.063\n681\t0.375\n682\t0.181\n683\t\n684\t0.110\n685\t0.100\n686\t0.379\n687\t0.315\n688\t\n689\t0.637\n690\t0.619\n691\t0.558\n692\t0.616\n693\t\n694\t0.632\n695\t0.606\n696\t0.545\n697\t0.616\n698\t\n699\t0.831\n700\t0.811\n701\t0.757\n702\t0.809\n703\t\n704\tACE-Step 1.5\n705\tACE-Step XL-Turbo\n706\tMusicGen-Large\n707\tStable Audio 3 Medium\n708\t\n709\t2.526\n710\t3.036\n711\t2.488\n712\t2.123\n713\t\n714\tReference layout\n715\tGenerated layout\n716\t\n717\t2.149 0.712\n718\t2.303 0.699\n719\t\n720\t0.169\n721\t0.140\n722\t\n723\t0.305 0.087 0.635 0.836\n724\t0.287 0.804 0.630 0.830\n725\t\n726\tMatched-data ACE-Step 1.5-FT (no layout) 2.740 0.877\n727\tcontrols\n728\tShuffled-layout training\n729\t2.772 0.734\n730\tShuffled-layout inference\n731\t2.192 0.740\n732\t\n733\t0.153\n734\t0.144\n735\t0.165\n736\t\n737\t0.234 0.351 0.560 0.736\n738\t0.267 0.136 0.599 0.807\n739\t0.293 0.117 0.635 0.834\n740\t\n741\tOur model\n742\t\n743\t0.797 0.152\n744\t0.756 0.174\n745\t0.825 0.180\n746\t0.726 0.229\n747\t\n748\t0.284\n749\t0.300\n750\t0.280\n751\t0.378\n752\t\n753\t0.588\n754\t0.566\n755\t0.517\n756\t0.573\n757\t\n758\t0.828\n759\t0.801\n760\t0.746\n761\t0.812\n762\t\n763\tBaselines\n764\t\n765\tACE-Step 1.5\n766\tACE-Step XL-Turbo\n767\tMusicGen-Large\n768\tStable Audio 3 Medium\n769\t\n770\t2.480 0.685 0.129\n771\t1.994 0.617 0.147\n772\t3.279 0.717 0.156\n773\t2.007 0.662 0.177\n774\t\n775\t0.289 0.186\n776\t0.317 0.611\n777\t0.241 0.215\n778\t0.365 0.132\n779\t\n780\t0.797\n781\t0.779\n782\t0.756\n783\t0.794\n784\t\n785\tOur model\n786\t\n787\tGenerated layout\n788\t\n789\t3.456 0.671\n790\t\n791\t0.116\n792\t\n793\t0.285 0.655 0.594 0.809\n794\t\n795\tMatched-data ACE-Step 1.5-FT (no layout) 3.124 0.844\n796\tcontrols\n797\tShuffled-layout training\n798\t3.714 0.670\n799\t\n800\t0.123\n801\t0.120\n802\t\n803\t0.222 1.458 0.535 0.726\n804\t0.286 0.626 0.600 0.820\n805\t\n806\tTable 3: Objective results. CLAP, KL, and SCM abbreviate CLAPScore, PaSST-KL, and SCM\n807\tEnergy Distance. Bold and underline mark the first- and second-ranked numerical values per dataset,\n808\twith rounded ties bold. The three matched-data controls remove MusicLayout or mismatch it in\n809\ttraining or inference.\n810\tWith model-generated layouts, our model also shows strong structural organization without\n811\taccess to the target-derived layout used in the reference-layout condition. On FreeMIDI, both\n812\tboundary scores are numerically above all baselines. On MidiCaps, F3.0 is numerically above all\n813\tbaselines, while F0.5 remains competitive. On MuChin, both boundary scores are numerically\n814\tabove all baselines, showing that this advantage persists under an acoustic-domain shift from MIDIsynthesized training data to accompaniments separated from real recordings. These results indicate\n815\tthat explicit intermediate planning improves the organization of long-range musical structure in\n816\tend-to-end generation.\n817\tThe comparison with the matched-data no-layout control further isolates this structural benefit.\n818\tBoth layout conditions have higher boundary scores than the no-layout control wherever available,\n819\tand the generated-layout condition retains this advantage across all three datasets. Thus, under the\n820\tsame MIDI-synthesized training data, explicit layout planning improves boundary agreement in\n821\tboth controlled and automatic generation.\n822\tSCM Energy Distance measures agreement between dataset-level structural complexity distribu-\n823\t\n824\t8\n825\t\n826\t\ftions rather than song-level layout correctness. On the two MIDI-derived evaluation sets, our model\n827\tin the reference-layout condition records the lowest observed SCM Energy Distance among the\n828\tnon-ablation systems , indicating that the specified layouts are realized with corpus-level structural\n829\tcomplexity close to the references. On MuChin, the SCM Energy Distance in the generated-layout\n830\tcondition is comparatively high. This weaker agreement is consistent with the training–evaluation\n831\tshift from MIDI-synthesized instrumental music to accompaniments separated from real vocal\n832\trecordings, which have substantially different structural-complexity distributions. However, with\n833\ttraining data controlled, the generated-layout condition has lower SCM than the no-layout condition.\n834\tThis comparison suggests a structural benefit from layout planning.\n835\t\n836\tPrompt A\n837\tPrompt A\n838\tPrompt B\n839\tPrompt A\n840\tLayout A (GT) Layout A (Gen) Layout B (GT) Layout B (Gen)\n841\t\n842\tFree 1\n843\t\n844\tFree 2\n845\t\n846\tMidi 1\n847\t\n848\tMidi 2\n849\t\n850\tFigure 3: Structural effects of layout manipulation for two FreeMIDI and two MidiCaps examples.\n851\tEach row presents, from left to right, the target GT under Prompt A and Layout A, generation\n852\tunder the same prompt and reference layout, the layout-donor GT under Prompt B and Layout\n853\tB, and generation from Prompt A paired with Layout B. For clear visualization, SSM intensities\n854\tare normalized independently within each panel. Darker regions within a panel indicate stronger\n855\trecurrence. Audio signals in each row are cropped to their common minimum duration.\n856\t\n857\t9\n858\t\n859\t\fStructural Effects of Layout Manipulation\n860\tFigure 3 presents four separate layout-manipulation cases, with two drawn from FreeMIDI and two\n861\tfrom MidiCaps. These cases examine how layout manipulation changes the generated structure\n862\twhile the text prompt is held fixed. In each row, item A serves as the target and provides Prompt\n863\tA and its reference Layout A. A different item B supplies Layout B, which we refer to as the\n864\tdonor layout. The SSM for item B is included to visualize the recurrence structure associated\n865\twith Layout B. We refer to generation from Prompt A and Layout A as the matched generation,\n866\tand generation from the same prompt and Layout B as the manipulated generation. In these\n867\texamples, the matched generation shows recurrence patterns similar to the target reference, while\n868\tthe manipulated generation loses some target-aligned patterns and exhibits patterns closer to the\n869\tdonor reference. Because the manipulated generation combines the target prompt with the donor\n870\tlayout, it can retain structural characteristics associated with the target while adopting aspects of\n871\tthe donor’s organization. Together, these cases illustrate that MusicLayout can be manipulated\n872\tbefore audio generation to control the structure of the generated music.\n873\tFine-Grained Regional Controls\n874\tWe further tested three direct edits to a single 240-second MusicLayout, holding the text prompt\n875\tand sampling seeds fixed so that each controlled condition differed from the original only in the\n876\tdeclared MusicLayout fields. Figure 4 compares the original and controlled audio over the affected\n877\tinterval.\n878\tFirst, in the 0–11-second introduction, we replaced sparse, high-energy strings and synthesizer\n879\tstrings with dense, high-energy drums and sparse, high-energy bass. This edit was expected to\n880\tadd stronger low-frequency rhythmic content and broadband percussive transients. The controlled\n881\tspectrogram shows both effects: energy below 250 Hz becomes dominant and repeated vertical\n882\ttransients appear across the band.\n883\tSecond, over 142–196 seconds, we changed every active role to sparse density and medium energy\n884\twhile leaving the segment boundaries, labels, families, and instrument identities unchanged. The\n885\texpected result was a less dense and less intense realization of the same region. Relative to the\n886\toriginal, the controlled excerpt has visibly reduced broadband activity and its RMS level decreases\n887\tby 15% (1.4 dB).\n888\tThird, we preserved the complete 0–91-second prefix and rewrote the remaining form from a\n889\tlargely repeated chorus sequence into a breakdown, bridge, build, solo, chorus, and outro, together\n890\twith their associated roles and recurrence families. The expected result was a new long-range\n891\tprogression after the preserved prefix. The controlled spectrogram exhibits the intended succession\n892\tof contrasting regimes, including the reduced texture of the breakdown and bridge, the subsequent\n893\tbuild, and the denser solo and chorus. Thus, all three edits produce changes consistent with their\n894\tspecified local or regional controls.\n895\tAblation Study\n896\tThe three matched-data controls in Table 3 form a progressive ablation of MusicLayout. First, the\n897\tfinetuned ACE-Step 1.5 control removes MusicLayout entirely. Second, shuffled-layout training\n898\tintroduces MusicLayout but pairs each training item with a layout from another item, so the model\n899\tlearns from mismatched layout–audio pairs. At inference, this model generates its own layout\n900\tfrom the prompt before audio generation, following the same end-to-end procedure as the full\n901\tgenerated-layout condition. Third, shuffled-layout inference restores correctly paired layouts during\n902\ttraining but replaces the conditioning layout with one from another item at inference.\n903\t10\n904\t\n905\t\f(a) Original\n906\t\n907\tControlled\n908\t\n909\tA2\n910\t\n911\tA2\n912\t\n913\t4\n914\t−40\n915\t\n916\t2\n917\t1\n918\t\n919\tA1\n920\t\n921\tA1\n922\t\n923\t0.5\n924\t0.1\n925\t\n926\t−80\n927\t2\n928\t\n929\tFrequency (kHz)\n930\t\n931\t16\n932\t\n933\t4\n934\t\n935\t6\n936\t\n937\t8\n938\t\n939\t10\n940\t\n941\t0\n942\t\n943\t2\n944\t\n945\t4\n946\t\n947\t6\n948\t\n949\t8\n950\t\n951\tTime (s)\n952\t\n953\tTime (s)\n954\t\n955\t(b) Original\n956\t\n957\tControlled\n958\t\n959\tB\n960\t\n961\t10\n962\t\n963\t0\n964\t\n965\tB\n966\t\n967\t8\n968\t4\n969\t−40\n970\t\n971\t2\n972\t1\n973\t0.5\n974\t0.1\n975\t\n976\t−80\n977\t150\n978\t\n979\tFrequency (kHz)\n980\t\n981\tdB (pair reference)\n982\t\n983\t0\n984\t\n985\t16\n986\t\n987\tdB (pair reference)\n988\t\n989\t0\n990\t\n991\t8\n992\t\n993\tC1\n994\t\n995\t160\n996\t\n997\t170\n998\t\n999\t180\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "b6358724-e349-47d8-9c24-ec80d9e01c36", "uuid": "6143a5a6-0eab-48d0-b612-ce69794d9252", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T18:30:52.179Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/2608.09035v1.txt", "content": "\nExperiments\n\nWe evaluate generation, structural control, layout manipulation, ablations, and subjective quality\nagainst representative text-to-music systems. For our framework, we consider two layout conditions.\nIn the reference-layout condition, the model receives a MusicLayout derived from the target music.\n5\n\n\fDataset\n\nAudio source\n\nTrain Eval.\n\nFreeMIDI MIDI-synth.\n24,474 2,719\nMidiCaps MIDI-synth.\n– 1,040\nMuChin Real, separated accomp.\n– 1,000\n\nTable 2: Datasets used in our experiments.\nThis condition jointly tests whether MusicLayout captures the target’s musical organization and\nwhether the model can use it to control the structure of the generated audio. In the generated-layout\ncondition, the model predicts a layout from the text prompt before generating audio, evaluating the\ncomplete end-to-end text-to-music process.\n\n4.1\n\nExperimental Setup\n\nDatasets. We used three datasets with complementary roles. FreeMIDI [12] provided the training\ndata and a disjoint in-domain evaluation set. MidiCaps [24] was used to evaluate generalization to\nout-of-domain MIDI, while MuChin [38] provided an out-of-domain evaluation on real audio. Their\ndetails are summarized in Table 2.\nFreeMIDI. We collected a subset of FreeMIDI and retained only pieces longer than 15 seconds.\nFor each remaining MIDI file, we extracted a MusicLayout and synthesized its audio, forming\na layout–audio pair. We then provided the audio to MOSS-Music [27], an audio-understanding\nlanguage model, to produce the generation prompt, yielding aligned prompt–layout–audio triples.\nMidiCaps. MidiCaps already provides natural-language captions. We selected a subset with a\nbalanced distribution of style labels and removed MIDI files that overlap with FreeMIDI. For each\nremaining piece, we extracted a layout and synthesized audio from the MIDI, directly pairing both\nwith its caption to form evaluation triples.\nMuChin. MuChin contains vocal music with Chinese captions but no MIDI, and thus cannot\nsupport the reference-layout condition. We used DeepSeek-V4-Flash [40] to translate the captions\ninto English and remove vocal-related descriptions. We further separated the accompaniment\nfrom the vocals using a community-trained BS-RoFormer model [22] and used only the separated\naccompaniment for metric computation. Generation conditions that require an externally supplied\nlayout—the reference-layout and shuffled-layout-inference conditions—are not reported on MuChin.\nCompared systems. We compared against MusicGen [4], ACE-Step 1.5 and ACE-Step XL-Turbo\n[13], and Stable Audio 3 Medium [10]. We evaluated our model under either the generated-layout\nor the reference-layout condition. To control for the effect of additional training data, we also\nfinetuned ACE-Step 1.5 on the same data using the same LM adaptation setup as our model. We\ndenote this matched-data no-layout control as ACE-Step 1.5-FT. It directly predicts audio from the\nconditioning context c without MusicLayout tokens, serving as both the primary fair comparison\nfor explicit layout planning and the first step of the progressive ablation. Systems shared evaluation\nitems, prompts, and seeds when supported, and baselines used officially recommended decoding\nsettings. Systems without a layout representation targeted each item’s reference duration. For\nlong-form MusicGen generation, we followed its official continuation mechanism. For our model,\nthe generation duration is instead determined by the end time of the conditioning layout. In the\ngenerated-layout condition, this duration is therefore predicted by the model rather than specified\nexternally, whereas the reference-layout condition matches the reference duration because its layout\nis derived from the target music.\n\n6\n\n\fEvaluation protocol. We report Fréchet Audio Distance (FAD) [30] for acoustic distribution\nsimilarity, PaSST-KL [17] for sound-event agreement, SSIM [37] for local time–frequency similarity,\nand CLAPScore [7] for text–audio alignment.\nFor long-range structure, SCM Energy Distance [5, 33] compares corpus-level structural complexity distributions, while F0.5 and F3.0 acoustic-boundary agreement [36] measure fine- and\ncoarse-grained transition alignment within each piece. To examine how layout manipulation changes\nlong-range organization, we also visualize recurrence-based self-similarity matrices (SSMs), following\nthe structural visualization used in Stable Audio 2 and its underlying music structure analysis\nmethod [9, 32].\nImplementation details. We finetuned ACE-Step 1.5’s 1.7B-parameter LM using six A800\nGPUs, a 4,096-token context, and an effective batch size of 48. We selected the checkpoint with the\nlowest development layout-to-audio loss. Layout and audio decoding used temperature/top-p values\nof 0.8/0.95 and 0.9/0.95.\n\n4.2\n\nResults\n\nOverall Generation Performance\nTable 3 summarizes the objective results. No single system dominates all aspects of generation. Our\nmodel with generated layouts records the lowest FAD and PaSST-KL on FreeMIDI. On MidiCaps, it\nalso records the lowest PaSST-KL, suggesting that layout-conditioned generation can remain close to\nthe reference acoustic-event distribution despite out-of-domain MIDI captions. Its lower CLAPScore\nrelative to Stable Audio and ACE-Step XL-Turbo, however, shows that explicit planning may not\nuniformly improve global text–audio correspondence.\nThe matched-data no-layout control provides a more direct test of the contribution of explicit\nlayout planning. It is weaker than the original ACE-Step 1.5 on nearly all metrics. This degradation\nlikely reflects the training-data difference: the original model was trained on high-quality real\nrecordings, whereas our finetuning data consists of MIDI-synthesized audio with lower audio quality\nand timbral fidelity, as well as a different acoustic distribution. Since our model and this control\nshare the same finetuning data and LM adaptation setup, their comparison isolates the effect of\nexplicit layout planning. Relative to this control, the reference-layout condition has more favorable\nvalues on all seven metrics for FreeMIDI and MidiCaps. The generated-layout condition does so\non six metrics for FreeMIDI and five for MidiCaps and MuChin. These results show that explicit\nlayout planning substantially improves generation under matched-data conditions and can partly\noffset the limitations of weaker training audio.\nOverall, our model is competitive on the two MIDI-derived datasets, indicating that explicit\nlayout planning contributes to overall music generation performance. Its FAD, PaSST-KL, and SSIM\nresults on MuChin are less favorable, however. This difference likely arises from an acoustic-domain\nshift: our model is trained on MIDI-synthesized instrumental audio, whereas MuChin consists of\naccompaniments separated from real recordings.\nStructural Control\nThe reference-layout condition records the highest boundary scores shown for FreeMIDI, the highest\nMidiCaps F3.0 , and a tie for the highest MidiCaps F0.5 . Because the reference layouts are derived\nfrom the target music, these results provide evidence that MusicLayout captures target organization\nand that conditioning on it can guide corresponding structure in the generated audio.\n\n7\n\n\fDataset\n\nSystem\n\nFAD↓\n\nKL↓ SSIM↑ CLAP↑ SCM↓ F0.5 ↑ F3.0 ↑\n\nACE-Step 1.5\nACE-Step XL-Turbo\nMusicGen-Large\nStable Audio 3 Medium\n\n2.874\n2.584\n3.706\n3.014\n\n0.789 0.159\n0.734 0.181\n0.834 0.189\n0.838 0.213\n\nReference layout\nGenerated layout\n\n2.610 0.740\n2.495 0.719\n\n0.183\n0.153\n\n0.238 0.054 0.645 0.838\n0.241 0.206 0.643 0.834\n\nMatched-data ACE-Step 1.5-FT (no layout) 3.345 0.956\ncontrols\nShuffled-layout training\n2.756 0.721\nShuffled-layout inference\n2.636 0.779\n\n0.154\n0.153\n0.176\n\n0.219 0.380 0.577 0.739\n0.216 0.100 0.607 0.810\n0.230 0.038 0.642 0.831\n\nGroup\n\nFreeMIDI Baselines\n\nOur model\n\nMidiCaps Baselines\n\nMuChin\n\n0.290\n0.313\n0.258\n0.352\n\n0.117\n0.063\n0.375\n0.181\n\n0.110\n0.100\n0.379\n0.315\n\n0.637\n0.619\n0.558\n0.616\n\n0.632\n0.606\n0.545\n0.616\n\n0.831\n0.811\n0.757\n0.809\n\nACE-Step 1.5\nACE-Step XL-Turbo\nMusicGen-Large\nStable Audio 3 Medium\n\n2.526\n3.036\n2.488\n2.123\n\nReference layout\nGenerated layout\n\n2.149 0.712\n2.303 0.699\n\n0.169\n0.140\n\n0.305 0.087 0.635 0.836\n0.287 0.804 0.630 0.830\n\nMatched-data ACE-Step 1.5-FT (no layout) 2.740 0.877\ncontrols\nShuffled-layout training\n2.772 0.734\nShuffled-layout inference\n2.192 0.740\n\n0.153\n0.144\n0.165\n\n0.234 0.351 0.560 0.736\n0.267 0.136 0.599 0.807\n0.293 0.117 0.635 0.834\n\nOur model\n\n0.797 0.152\n0.756 0.174\n0.825 0.180\n0.726 0.229\n\n0.284\n0.300\n0.280\n0.378\n\n0.588\n0.566\n0.517\n0.573\n\n0.828\n0.801\n0.746\n0.812\n\nBaselines\n\nACE-Step 1.5\nACE-Step XL-Turbo\nMusicGen-Large\nStable Audio 3 Medium\n\n2.480 0.685 0.129\n1.994 0.617 0.147\n3.279 0.717 0.156\n2.007 0.662 0.177\n\n0.289 0.186\n0.317 0.611\n0.241 0.215\n0.365 0.132\n\n0.797\n0.779\n0.756\n0.794\n\nOur model\n\nGenerated layout\n\n3.456 0.671\n\n0.116\n\n0.285 0.655 0.594 0.809\n\nMatched-data ACE-Step 1.5-FT (no layout) 3.124 0.844\ncontrols\nShuffled-layout training\n3.714 0.670\n\n0.123\n0.120\n\n0.222 1.458 0.535 0.726\n0.286 0.626 0.600 0.820\n\nTable 3: Objective results. CLAP, KL, and SCM abbreviate CLAPScore, PaSST-KL, and SCM\nEnergy Distance. Bold and underline mark the first- and second-ranked numerical values per dataset,\nwith rounded ties bold. The three matched-data controls remove MusicLayout or mismatch it in\ntraining or inference.\nWith model-generated layouts, our model also shows strong structural organization without\naccess to the target-derived layout used in the reference-layout condition. On FreeMIDI, both\nboundary scores are numerically above all baselines. On MidiCaps, F3.0 is numerically above all\nbaselines, while F0.5 remains competitive. On MuChin, both boundary scores are numerically\nabove all baselines, showing that this advantage persists under an acoustic-domain shift from MIDIsynthesized training data to accompaniments separated from real recordings. These results indicate\nthat explicit intermediate planning improves the organization of long-range musical structure in\nend-to-end generation.\nThe comparison with the matched-data no-layout control further isolates this structural benefit.\nBoth layout conditions have higher boundary scores than the no-layout control wherever available,\nand the generated-layout condition retains this advantage across all three datasets. Thus, under the\nsame MIDI-synthesized training data, explicit layout planning improves boundary agreement in\nboth controlled and automatic generation.\nSCM Energy Distance measures agreement between dataset-level structural complexity distribu-\n\n8\n\n\ftions rather than song-level layout correctness. On the two MIDI-derived evaluation sets, our model\nin the reference-layout condition records the lowest observed SCM Energy Distance among the\nnon-ablation systems , indicating that the specified layouts are realized with corpus-level structural\ncomplexity close to the references. On MuChin, the SCM Energy Distance in the generated-layout\ncondition is comparatively high. This weaker agreement is consistent with the training–evaluation\nshift from MIDI-synthesized instrumental music to accompaniments separated from real vocal\nrecordings, which have substantially different structural-complexity distributions. However, with\ntraining data controlled, the generated-layout condition has lower SCM than the no-layout condition.\nThis comparison suggests a structural benefit from layout planning.\n\nPrompt A\nPrompt A\nPrompt B\nPrompt A\nLayout A (GT) Layout A (Gen) Layout B (GT) Layout B (Gen)\n\nFree 1\n\nFree 2\n\nMidi 1\n\nMidi 2\n\nFigure 3: Structural effects of layout manipulation for two FreeMIDI and two MidiCaps examples.\nEach row presents, from left to right, the target GT under Prompt A and Layout A, generation\nunder the same prompt and reference layout, the layout-donor GT under Prompt B and Layout\nB, and generation from Prompt A paired with Layout B. For clear visualization, SSM intensities\nare normalized independently within each panel. Darker regions within a panel indicate stronger\nrecurrence. Audio signals in each row are cropped to their common minimum duration.\n\n9\n\n\fStructural Effects of Layout Manipulation\nFigure 3 presents four separate layout-manipulation cases, with two drawn from FreeMIDI and two\nfrom MidiCaps. These cases examine how layout manipulation changes the generated structure\nwhile the text prompt is held fixed. In each row, item A serves as the target and provides Prompt\nA and its reference Layout A. A different item B supplies Layout B, which we refer to as the\ndonor layout. The SSM for item B is included to visualize the recurrence structure associated\nwith Layout B. We refer to generation from Prompt A and Layout A as the matched generation,\nand generation from the same prompt and Layout B as the manipulated generation. In these\nexamples, the matched generation shows recurrence patterns similar to the target reference, while\nthe manipulated generation loses some target-aligned patterns and exhibits patterns closer to the\ndonor reference. Because the manipulated generation combines the target prompt with the donor\nlayout, it can retain structural characteristics associated with the target while adopting aspects of\nthe donor’s organization. Together, these cases illustrate that MusicLayout can be manipulated\nbefore audio generation to control the structure of the generated music.\nFine-Grained Regional Controls\nWe further tested three direct edits to a single 240-second MusicLayout, holding the text prompt\nand sampling seeds fixed so that each controlled condition differed from the original only in the\ndeclared MusicLayout fields. Figure 4 compares the original and controlled audio over the affected\ninterval.\nFirst, in the 0–11-second introduction, we replaced sparse, high-energy strings and synthesizer\nstrings with dense, high-energy drums and sparse, high-energy bass. This edit was expected to\nadd stronger low-frequency rhythmic content and broadband percussive transients. The controlled\nspectrogram shows both effects: energy below 250 Hz becomes dominant and repeated vertical\ntransients appear across the band.\nSecond, over 142–196 seconds, we changed every active role to sparse density and medium energy\nwhile leaving the segment boundaries, labels, families, and instrument identities unchanged. The\nexpected result was a less dense and less intense realization of the same region. Relative to the\noriginal, the controlled excerpt has visibly reduced broadband activity and its RMS level decreases\nby 15% (1.4 dB).\nThird, we preserved the complete 0–91-second prefix and rewrote the remaining form from a\nlargely repeated chorus sequence into a breakdown, bridge, build, solo, chorus, and outro, together\nwith their associated roles and recurrence families. The expected result was a new long-range\nprogression after the preserved prefix. The controlled spectrogram exhibits the intended succession\nof contrasting regimes, including the reduced texture of the breakdown and bridge, the subsequent\nbuild, and the denser solo and chorus. Thus, all three edits produce changes consistent with their\nspecified local or regional controls.\nAblation Study\nThe three matched-data controls in Table 3 form a progressive ablation of MusicLayout. First, the\nfinetuned ACE-Step 1.5 control removes MusicLayout entirely. Second, shuffled-layout training\nintroduces MusicLayout but pairs each training item with a layout from another item, so the model\nlearns from mismatched layout–audio pairs. At inference, this model generates its own layout\nfrom the prompt before audio generation, following the same end-to-end procedure as the full\ngenerated-layout condition. Third, shuffled-layout inference restores correctly paired layouts during\ntraining but replaces the conditioning layout with one from another item at inference.\n10\n\n\f(a) Original\n\nControlled\n\nA2\n\nA2\n\n4\n−40\n\n2\n1\n\nA1\n\nA1\n\n0.5\n0.1\n\n−80\n2\n\nFrequency (kHz)\n\n16\n\n4\n\n6\n\n8\n\n10\n\n0\n\n2\n\n4\n\n6\n\n8\n\nTime (s)\n\nTime (s)\n\n(b) Original\n\nControlled\n\nB\n\n10\n\n0\n\nB\n\n8\n4\n−40\n\n2\n1\n0.5\n0.1\n\n−80\n150\n\nFrequency (kHz)\n\ndB (pair reference)\n\n0\n\n16\n\ndB (pair reference)\n\n0\n\n8\n\nC1\n\n160\n\n170\n\n180", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 500, "totalLines": 2074}}}
