{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8779c8ce26d87292", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1000\t\n1001\t190\n1002\t\n1003\t150\n1004\t\n1005\t160\n1006\t\n1007\t170\n1008\t\n1009\t180\n1010\t\n1011\tTime (s)\n1012\t\n1013\tTime (s)\n1014\t\n1015\t(c) Original\n1016\t\n1017\tControlled\n1018\t\n1019\tC2\n1020\t\n1021\tC3\n1022\t\n1023\tC1\n1024\t\n1025\tC2\n1026\t\n1027\t190\n1028\t\n1029\t0\n1030\t\n1031\tC3\n1032\t\n1033\t8\n1034\t4\n1035\t−40\n1036\t\n1037\t2\n1038\t1\n1039\t0.5\n1040\t0.1\n1041\t\n1042\tdB (pair reference)\n1043\t\n1044\tFrequency (kHz)\n1045\t\n1046\t16\n1047\t\n1048\t−80\n1049\t100\n1050\t\n1051\t120\n1052\t\n1053\t140\n1054\t\n1055\t160\n1056\t\n1057\t180\n1058\t\n1059\t200\n1060\t\n1061\t220\n1062\t\n1063\t240\n1064\t\n1065\t100\n1066\t\n1067\tTime (s)\n1068\t\n1069\t120\n1070\t\n1071\t140\n1072\t\n1073\t160\n1074\t\n1075\t180\n1076\t\n1077\t200\n1078\t\n1079\t220\n1080\t\n1081\t240\n1082\t\n1083\tTime (s)\n1084\t\n1085\tFigure 4: Paired log-mel spectrograms for three fine-grained MusicLayout controls: (a) role\n1086\treplacement over 0–11 seconds, (b) reduced density and energy over 142–196 seconds, and (c)\n1087\ta form rewrite over 91–240 seconds. Light-blue boxes use matched time–frequency coordinates:\n1088\tA1/A2 mark low-frequency and broadband changes, B marks reduced broadband activity, and\n1089\tC1–C3 mark successive stages of the form rewrite. Each pair shares its 0-dB reference and 80-dB\n1090\trange. Intensities are comparable within a row. Time axes retain full-song coordinates.\n1091\tShuffled-layout training has favorable values on five of seven metrics relative to the no-layout\n1092\tcontrol for each of the three datasets. This pattern suggests that layout-shaped supervision may\n1093\tretain a generic structural benefit even when item-level information is incorrect. On the two\n1094\tMIDI-synthesized datasets, our full model with generated layouts has favorable values on five\n1095\tof seven metrics relative to shuffled-layout training for both FreeMIDI and MidiCaps. Because\n1096\tboth conditions generate layouts at inference, this pattern suggests that correct layout–audio\n1097\tcorrespondence during training provides information beyond the presence of layout-shaped tokens\n1098\tand can improve long-form music generation when the evaluation domain matches the type of audio\n1099\tused for training. On MuChin, the two conditions obtain broadly similar results. This may result\n1100\tfrom the acoustic-domain shift discussed above, since our model was trained on MIDI-synthesized\n1101\taudio rather than accompaniments from real recordings.\n1102\tHolding correctly paired training fixed, the reference-layout condition has favorable FAD, KL,\n1103\tSSIM, CLAPScore, and F3.0 values relative to shuffled-layout inference on both MIDI-derived\n1104\tdatasets. This indicates that item-matched layouts provide a more informative inference-time\n1105\t\n1106\t11\n1107\t\n1108\t\fAll (N = 59)\n1109\tSystem\n1110\t\n1111\tF\n1112\t\n1113\tM\n1114\t\n1115\tExp. (N = 36)\n1116\tT\n1117\t\n1118\tF\n1119\t\n1120\tM\n1121\t\n1122\tT\n1123\t\n1124\tGT\n1125\t3.200 3.490 3.408 3.000 3.361 3.300\n1126\tACE 1.5\n1127\t3.283 3.329 3.225 3.031 3.342 3.108\n1128\tACE-Step 1.5-FT 2.931 2.632 2.702 2.631 2.386 2.661\n1129\tOurs-Ref\n1130\t3.064 3.012 3.093 2.989 2.917 3.183\n1131\tOurs-Gen\n1132\t2.914 2.586 2.869 2.733 2.783 2.839\n1133\t\n1134\tTable 4: Subjective ratings on FreeMIDI and MidiCaps (0–5). F/M/T denote fidelity, musical\n1135\timpression, and text consistency. Exp. denotes experienced listeners, and FT denotes matched-data\n1136\tfinetuning.\n1137\tSystem\n1138\t\n1139\tScore\n1140\t\n1141\tACE-Step 1.5\n1142\t2.319\n1143\tACE-Step 1.5-FT\n1144\t1.444\n1145\tOurs (reference layout) 2.556\n1146\tOurs (generated layout) 2.044\n1147\t\n1148\tTable 5: GT-reference structural similarity rated by music-experienced participants on the same\n1149\tMIDI-derived items (0–5).\n1150\tconditioning signal than a plausible but unrelated layout.\n1151\tA shuffled layout remains musically plausible because it comes from a real piece and retains valid\n1152\tsections, timing, repetitions, and arrangements. Shuffling can therefore preserve the corpus-level\n1153\tlayout distribution while breaking item-level correspondence, so low SCM alone does not demonstrate\n1154\tcorrect layout control.\n1155\t\n1156\t4.3\n1157\t\n1158\tSubjective Evaluation\n1159\t\n1160\tTo enable a direct comparison with the reference-layout condition, we excluded MuChin because it\n1161\tdoes not provide reference layouts. We sampled 10 items each from FreeMIDI and MidiCaps and\n1162\tassigned them repeatedly to 27 participants, including 15 with prior music experience. Participants\n1163\trated audio fidelity, musical impression, and text consistency on a 0–5 scale, with one decimal place\n1164\tallowed. Each evaluation compared all five systems, yielding 59 ratings per system, including 36\n1165\tratings from experienced listeners.\n1166\tTable 4 shows that comparisons with the matched-data no-layout control vary with layout\n1167\tcondition and listener experience. Across all participants, the reference-layout condition has higher\n1168\tobserved scores on all three dimensions. Generated-layout scores are broadly comparable, with\n1169\tsimilar fidelity and musical impression but higher text consistency. Within the experienced subset,\n1170\tboth layout conditions score numerically higher than the control across all dimensions. Notably,\n1171\tthe reference-layout condition also has higher observed text consistency than the original ACEStep 1.5 among experienced listeners (3.183 vs. 3.108), suggesting that an explicit MusicLayout can\n1172\tprovide an additional control signal for steering music generation. This pattern may arise because\n1173\tmusic-experienced listeners are more attentive to structural organization when evaluating generated\n1174\tmusic. ACE-Step 1.5 has the highest observed fidelity score across the five systems (3.283), above\n1175\tGT (3.200). As discussed above, this difference is consistent with the acoustic-quality gap between\n1176\tthe MIDI-synthesized data in our experiments and the real recordings used to train ACE-Step 1.5.\n1177\tIt underscores the need for matched-data controls to isolate layout planning from training-data\n1178\tdifferences.\n1179\t12\n1180\t\n1181\t\fUsing the same dataset scope, we conducted a follow-up on a subset of the previously sampled\n1182\titems. A subset of the experienced participants was shown the GT reference for each item and asked\n1183\tto rate the structural similarity of the remaining outputs to the references. The reference-layout\n1184\tcondition received a higher observed structural similarity than ACE-Step 1.5, providing subjective\n1185\tevidence that MusicLayout captures reference organization and carries it into generated audio. The\n1186\tgenerated-layout condition was slightly weaker because coarse dataset prompts do not uniquely\n1187\tspecify the GT layout. Once predicted, the layout steers generation toward its own plausible\n1188\tstructure. The objective structural metrics above suggest that these outputs can remain structurally\n1189\torganized even when they do not reproduce the particular GT structure.\n1190\t\n1191\t5\n1192\t\n1193\tConclusion\n1194\t\n1195\tWe presented MusicLayout, an explicit, time-aligned representation of section organization, development, and instrument arrangement for a unified autoregressive audio LM. Generated before audio\n1196\ttokens, it makes structural planning inspectable and adjustable. The same LM plans the layout and\n1197\tpredicts audio tokens conditioned on it while the synthesis components remain frozen. Our results\n1198\tsuggest that MusicLayout provides an interpretable interface that supports long-range structural\n1199\torganization and layout-level control.\n1200\tDespite these benefits, MusicLayout adjusts structural plans only before synthesis and cannot\n1201\tedit existing audio or regenerate selected regions. Our reliance on MIDI-synthesized training audio\n1202\tmay also limit audio fidelity. Future work will pursue finer control and higher-quality, structurally\n1203\taligned audio data.\n1204\t\n1205\tAcknowledgments\n1206\tThis work was supported by Ant Group.\n1207\t\n1208\tA\n1209\t\n1210\tMusicLayout Representation and Annotation\n1211\t\n1212\tA.1\n1213\t\n1214\tSerialization Grammar\n1215\t\n1216\tMusicLayout is serialized as a discrete text sequence with one optional piece-level family block\n1217\tand one required segment block. A family records a piece-local identifier, its member segments, its\n1218\tcardinality, and whether the members are adjacent. Each segment records an integer-second time\n1219\tspan, a functional section label, a texture, an arrangement-change label, its family membership and\n1220\tfamily role, the degree of variation from the family prototype, a repetition flag, and a list of active\n1221\tinstrument tuples. The grammar used in all reported experiments is summarized below:\n1222\t<layout>\n1223\t<families>\n1224\t<fam> id=fam_K members=sI,sJ count=N adj=true|false </fam>\n1225\t</families>\n1226\t<segments>\n1227\t<seg> id=sI time=A-B label=L texture=T change=C\n1228\tfam=fam_K fam_role=R variation=V repeat=true|false\n1229\t<roles> instrument:register:density:energy | ... </roles>\n1230\t</seg>\n1231\t</segments>\n1232\t</layout>\n1233\t\n1234\t13\n1235\t\n1236\t\fField\n1237\t\n1238\tAllowed values\n1239\t\n1240\tSection label\n1241\t\n1242\tintro, verse, prechorus, chorus, bridge, breakdown, outro, transition, hook,\n1243\tsolo, build\n1244\tlayered, rhythm_driven, melodic_front, harmonic_bed, percussive,\n1245\tbuild_up, sparse_pulse, lead_front, contrast, other\n1246\tentry, continuation, lift, drop, contrast, outro\n1247\tintro_anchor, primary_repeat, secondary_repeat, adjacent_variant,\n1248\toutro_anchor, unique\n1249\tunique, same, light_var, strong_var\n1250\tlow, mid, high\n1251\tsparse, med, dense\n1252\tlow, med, high\n1253\t\n1254\tTexture\n1255\tChange\n1256\tFamily role\n1257\tVariation\n1258\tRegister\n1259\tDensity\n1260\tEnergy\n1261\t\n1262\tTable 6: Closed structural and arrangement vocabularies in MusicLayout.\n1263\tFamily identifiers are local to a piece rather than global semantic labels. For example, fam_0\n1264\tdenotes one recurring material within the current piece and may refer to unrelated material in another\n1265\tpiece. The extraction pipeline retains singleton material as an explicitly declared one-member\n1266\tfamily with count=1 and adj=true. Its segment uses variation=unique and repeat=false. For\n1267\ta recurring family, the first chronological member serves as its variation prototype and is marked\n1268\tsame. Later members are marked same, light_var, or strong_var according to their distance from\n1269\tthat prototype.\n1270\t\n1271\tA.2\n1272\t\n1273\tClosed Vocabularies\n1274\t\n1275\tTable 6 gives the structural vocabularies. The change field describes the segment’s arrangementlevel relation to its predecessor, whereas fam_role describes its role in the piece-level recurrence\n1276\torganization. These fields are distinct from the functional section label.\n1277\tThe instrument field uses 25 compact categories: acoustic_piano, electric_piano, plucked_\n1278\tkeyboard, mallet_bell, organ, accordion_harmonica, acoustic_guitar, electric_guitar,\n1279\tbass, strings, orchestral, synth_strings, choir_voice, brass, sax, woodwind, synth_lead,\n1280\tsynth_pad, synth_fx, world_plucked, percussion, reverse_cymbal, sound_fx, drums, and\n1281\tother. General MIDI program numbers are mapped deterministically to these categories. Instrument entries retain the segment-local register, density, and energy attributes. These attributes\n1282\tare defined at the instrument tuple level rather than duplicated as separate segment-level fields.\n1283\t\n1284\tA.3\n1285\t\n1286\tVariation Strength\n1287\t\n1288\tFor each recurring family, we compare every later segment with the first family member. Let dr be\n1289\tthe Jaccard distance between active-role sets, and let de , dd , and da be normalized differences in\n1290\tenergy, note density, and active-role count. The base distance is\n1291\td = 0.45dr + 0.20de + 0.20dd + 0.15da .\n1292\t\n1293\t(4)\n1294\t\n1295\tThe implementation adds small deterministic adjustments when coarse energy, density, texture,\n1296\tarrangement-change, repeat-neighbor, or source-relation evidence differs from the family prototype.\n1297\tWe serialize scores at most 0.15 as same, scores in (0.15, 0.40] as light_var, and larger scores as\n1298\tstrong_var. This field therefore records an interpretable, rule-derived degree of variation rather\n1299\tthan a human rating.\n1300\t14\n1301\t\n1302\t\fA.4\n1303\t\n1304\tValidation\n1305\t\n1306\tBefore a generated layout can condition audio decoding, the parser checks the outer layout and\n1307\tsegment wrappers, unique segment identifiers, nondecreasing integer-second start times, positive\n1308\tspans, valid closed-vocabulary values, four-field instrument tuples, family-member counts, and\n1309\treferences from segments to declared families. It also rejects legacy bar spans, legacy role tags,\n1310\tsegment-level energy/density fields, internal MIDI program slugs, and audio-code tokens inside the\n1311\tlayout. A failed layout is not silently repaired or rewritten.\n1312\t\n1313\tA.5\n1314\t\n1315\tAnnotation Pipeline\n1316\t\n1317\tThe annotation pipeline begins from aligned MIDI and rendered WAV files. It extracts bar-level\n1318\tactivity and instrument statistics, combines change and repetition evidence into time-aligned\n1319\tsegments, groups structurally related segments into piece-local material families, and derives the\n1320\tsection, texture, change, family, variation, and instrument attributes serialized above. MIDI\n1321\tprograms are used only as annotation evidence. The learning target remains audio-token generation.\n1322\tSegment spans are converted to audio time and rounded to integer seconds, with a minimum\n1323\tduration of one second after rounding.\n1324\tThe extraction procedure is deterministic once its feature and boundary settings are fixed.\n1325\tAlgorithm 1 summarizes its execution order. The symbolic representation supplies the structural\n1326\tevidence. The paired waveform is used to place the resulting spans on the rendered-audio timeline.\n1327\t\n1328\tB\n1329\t\n1330\tDataset Construction and Provenance\n1331\t\n1332\tB.1\n1333\t\n1334\tFreeMIDI\n1335\t\n1336\tFreeMIDI [12] supplies the training corpus and an in-domain evaluation set. We retained pieces\n1337\tlonger than 15 seconds, extracted one MusicLayout from each retained MIDI file, and synthesized\n1338\tthe aligned audio at 44.1 kHz using the FluidSynth [11] interface provided by PrettyMIDI [29],\n1339\twith MuseScore_General.sf2 [25]. Of 27,237 extracted items, 27,229 received a full-song caption\n1340\tfrom MOSS-Music-8B-Instruct [27]. Caption decoding was greedy. The prompt requested one\n1341\tconcise English paragraph grounded only in the audio, covering style, audible instrumentation and\n1342\troles, rhythm, texture, energy, musical development, and mood. It explicitly prohibited the use of\n1343\tfilenames, MIDI metadata, external labels, bullet lists, and unsupported claims.\n1344\tRequiring at least 15 seconds of audio and a nonempty supervised audio-code span retained\n1345\t27,198 items. Five additional items were removed because their complete planning sequences\n1346\texceeded the 4,096-token context. The resulting 27,193 items were split deterministically into\n1347\t24,474 training and 2,719 development items with seed 20260501. The development split is also the\n1348\tin-domain evaluation set.\n1349\t\n1350\tB.2\n1351\t\n1352\tMidiCaps\n1353\t\n1354\tMidiCaps [24] is an out-of-domain MIDI evaluation set. We first sampled 1,100 items with seed\n1355\t42 after requiring a duration of at least 120 seconds, at least two instruments, and a nonempty\n1356\tcaption. Genre-quota sampling followed by instrument-diverse greedy selection covered 40 source\n1357\tgenre labels. Because the labels are multi-valued, their frequencies need not sum to the number of\n1358\titems. The most frequent labels in the final set are electronic (581), pop (463), rock (208), classical\n1359\t(108), soundtrack (102), ambient (81), and jazz (59). Exact SHA-256 comparison of the MIDI files\n1360\tagainst the complete FreeMIDI corpus removed 60 overlaps, leaving 1,040 unique items. The source\n1361\t15\n1362\t\n1363\t\fAlgorithm 1 MusicLayout extraction from aligned symbolic music and audio\n1364\tRequire: MIDI performance M , aligned waveform duration D, and fixed extraction settings Θ\n1365\tEnsure: Valid serialized layout L, or rejection symbol ⊥\n1366\t1: function ExtractMusicLayout(M, D, Θ)\n1367\t2:\n1368\t(N, P, T, B, E) ← ParseSymbolic(M )\n1369\t▷ notes, programs, MIDI duration, bars, beats\n1370\t3:\n1371\tfor b ∈ B do\n1372\t4:\n1373\tfb ← AggregateBar(N, P, b)\n1374\t▷ density, energy, pitch, and six broad-role activities\n1375\t5:\n1376\tend for\n1377\t6:\n1378\tq ← AdjacentChangeScores(f )\n1379\t7:\n1380\tR ← BinarySegmentationCandidates(f, D)\n1381\t8:\n1382\tq ← AddBoundarySupport(q, R, f )\n1383\t9:\n1384\tn ← min(14, max(5, Round(D/28)))\n1385\t10:\n1386\tC ← SelectPeaks(q, mean(q) + 0.34 std(q), 3 bars, n)\n1387\t11:\n1388\tC ← RefineLongSpans(C, f, B)\n1389\t▷ adjacent-repeat and local-change tests\n1390\t12:\n1391\tC ← InsertMicroBoundaries(C, f, B)\n1392\t13:\n1393\tS ← ScaleBoundariesToAudio(C, B, T, D)\n1394\t14:\n1395\tS ← MergeShortSegments(S, 8 s)\n1396\t15:\n1397\tS ← RefineOpeningWithBeats(S, E, f, B, T, D)\n1398\t16:\n1399\tfor si ∈ S do\n1400\t17:\n1401\tzi ← AggregateSegment({fb : b ⊆ si })\n1402\t18:\n1403\tei ← AggregateInstrumentStatistics(N, P, si )\n1404\t19:\n1405\tend for\n1406\t20:\n1407\tF ←∅\n1408\t21:\n1409\tfor i = 1, . . . , |S| do\n1410\t▷ chronological greedy centroid assignment\n1411\t22:\n1412\tk∗ ← arg maxk cos(zi , µk )\n1413\t23:\n1414\tif F = ∅ or cos(zi , µk∗ ) < 0.965 then\n1415\t24:\n1416\tϕ(i) ← NewFamily(F, zi )\n1417\t25:\n1418\telse\n1419\t26:\n1420\tϕ(i) ← k∗ , µk∗ ← MemberMean({zj : ϕ(j) = k∗ })\n1421\t27:\n1422\tend if\n1423\t28:\n1424\tend for\n1425\t29:\n1426\tfor i, j ∈ {1, . . . , |S|} do\n1427\t30:\n1428\tAij ← cos(zi , zj )\n1429\t▷ relation evidence, not family assignment\n1430\t31:\n1431\tend for\n1432\t|S|\n1433\t32:\n1434\t(ℓi , ti , ci , ui )i=1 ← AssignDescriptors(S, z, A, F, ϕ)\n1435\t33:\n1436\tfor Fk ∈ F do\n1437\t34:\n1438\tpk ← min{i : ϕ(i) = k}\n1439\t35:\n1440\tfor i : ϕ(i) = k do\n1441\t36:\n1442\tvi ← VariationClass(si , spk , Θvariation )\n1443\t37:\n1444\tend for\n1445\t38:\n1446\tend for\n1447\t39:\n1448\tK ← MapProgramsToCategories(P )\n1449\t40:\n1450\tfor si ∈ S do\n1451\t41:\n1452\tri ← QuantizeInstrumentTuples(ei , K)\n1453\t42:\n1454\tend for\n1455\t|S|\n1456\t43:\n1457\tL ← SerializeAndRound(F, {si , ℓi , ti , ci , ui , vi , ri }i=1 )\n1458\t44:\n1459\tif ¬Validate(L) then\n1460\t45:\n1461\treturn ⊥\n1462\t46:\n1463\tend if\n1464\t47:\n1465\treturn L\n1466\t48: end function\n1467\t\n1468\tlocation and MIDI SHA-256 jointly define item identity. We then extracted MusicLayouts and\n1469\tsynthesized aligned audio using the same renderer as for FreeMIDI. Original MidiCaps captions\n1470\twere retained as prompts, and no MidiCaps item was used for training.\n1471\t\n1472\tB.3\n1473\t\n1474\tMuChin\n1475\t\n1476\tMuChin [38] provides an out-of-domain real-audio evaluation. Because it does not provide MIDI, it\n1477\tcannot supply a reference MusicLayout. We constructed a fixed 1,000-item manifest in the source\n1478\tmetadata order. For each item, DeepSeek-V4-Flash [40] received the original full-song description\n1479\tas context and a set of non-vocal tags covering instrumentation, arrangement, style, tempo, rhythm,\n1480\tand mood. The instruction required one concise English paragraph using only these safe musical\n1481\t16\n1482\t\n1483\t\fSetting\n1484\t\n1485\tStage 1\n1486\t\n1487\tStage 2\n1488\t\n1489\tTasks\n1490\tEpoch limit\n1491\tLearning rate\n1492\tMonitored development\n1493\ttask\n1494\tEarly-stopping patience\n1495\tTrainable scope\n1496\tLoss-bearing targets\n1497\t\n1498\tLayout planning\n1499\t10\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "b6358724-e349-47d8-9c24-ec80d9e01c36", "uuid": "f9962545-1079-4c65-ad6b-ca137420c06b", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T18:31:10.224Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/2608.09035v1.txt", "content": "\n190\n\n150\n\n160\n\n170\n\n180\n\nTime (s)\n\nTime (s)\n\n(c) Original\n\nControlled\n\nC2\n\nC3\n\nC1\n\nC2\n\n190\n\n0\n\nC3\n\n8\n4\n−40\n\n2\n1\n0.5\n0.1\n\ndB (pair reference)\n\nFrequency (kHz)\n\n16\n\n−80\n100\n\n120\n\n140\n\n160\n\n180\n\n200\n\n220\n\n240\n\n100\n\nTime (s)\n\n120\n\n140\n\n160\n\n180\n\n200\n\n220\n\n240\n\nTime (s)\n\nFigure 4: Paired log-mel spectrograms for three fine-grained MusicLayout controls: (a) role\nreplacement over 0–11 seconds, (b) reduced density and energy over 142–196 seconds, and (c)\na form rewrite over 91–240 seconds. Light-blue boxes use matched time–frequency coordinates:\nA1/A2 mark low-frequency and broadband changes, B marks reduced broadband activity, and\nC1–C3 mark successive stages of the form rewrite. Each pair shares its 0-dB reference and 80-dB\nrange. Intensities are comparable within a row. Time axes retain full-song coordinates.\nShuffled-layout training has favorable values on five of seven metrics relative to the no-layout\ncontrol for each of the three datasets. This pattern suggests that layout-shaped supervision may\nretain a generic structural benefit even when item-level information is incorrect. On the two\nMIDI-synthesized datasets, our full model with generated layouts has favorable values on five\nof seven metrics relative to shuffled-layout training for both FreeMIDI and MidiCaps. Because\nboth conditions generate layouts at inference, this pattern suggests that correct layout–audio\ncorrespondence during training provides information beyond the presence of layout-shaped tokens\nand can improve long-form music generation when the evaluation domain matches the type of audio\nused for training. On MuChin, the two conditions obtain broadly similar results. This may result\nfrom the acoustic-domain shift discussed above, since our model was trained on MIDI-synthesized\naudio rather than accompaniments from real recordings.\nHolding correctly paired training fixed, the reference-layout condition has favorable FAD, KL,\nSSIM, CLAPScore, and F3.0 values relative to shuffled-layout inference on both MIDI-derived\ndatasets. This indicates that item-matched layouts provide a more informative inference-time\n\n11\n\n\fAll (N = 59)\nSystem\n\nF\n\nM\n\nExp. (N = 36)\nT\n\nF\n\nM\n\nT\n\nGT\n3.200 3.490 3.408 3.000 3.361 3.300\nACE 1.5\n3.283 3.329 3.225 3.031 3.342 3.108\nACE-Step 1.5-FT 2.931 2.632 2.702 2.631 2.386 2.661\nOurs-Ref\n3.064 3.012 3.093 2.989 2.917 3.183\nOurs-Gen\n2.914 2.586 2.869 2.733 2.783 2.839\n\nTable 4: Subjective ratings on FreeMIDI and MidiCaps (0–5). F/M/T denote fidelity, musical\nimpression, and text consistency. Exp. denotes experienced listeners, and FT denotes matched-data\nfinetuning.\nSystem\n\nScore\n\nACE-Step 1.5\n2.319\nACE-Step 1.5-FT\n1.444\nOurs (reference layout) 2.556\nOurs (generated layout) 2.044\n\nTable 5: GT-reference structural similarity rated by music-experienced participants on the same\nMIDI-derived items (0–5).\nconditioning signal than a plausible but unrelated layout.\nA shuffled layout remains musically plausible because it comes from a real piece and retains valid\nsections, timing, repetitions, and arrangements. Shuffling can therefore preserve the corpus-level\nlayout distribution while breaking item-level correspondence, so low SCM alone does not demonstrate\ncorrect layout control.\n\n4.3\n\nSubjective Evaluation\n\nTo enable a direct comparison with the reference-layout condition, we excluded MuChin because it\ndoes not provide reference layouts. We sampled 10 items each from FreeMIDI and MidiCaps and\nassigned them repeatedly to 27 participants, including 15 with prior music experience. Participants\nrated audio fidelity, musical impression, and text consistency on a 0–5 scale, with one decimal place\nallowed. Each evaluation compared all five systems, yielding 59 ratings per system, including 36\nratings from experienced listeners.\nTable 4 shows that comparisons with the matched-data no-layout control vary with layout\ncondition and listener experience. Across all participants, the reference-layout condition has higher\nobserved scores on all three dimensions. Generated-layout scores are broadly comparable, with\nsimilar fidelity and musical impression but higher text consistency. Within the experienced subset,\nboth layout conditions score numerically higher than the control across all dimensions. Notably,\nthe reference-layout condition also has higher observed text consistency than the original ACEStep 1.5 among experienced listeners (3.183 vs. 3.108), suggesting that an explicit MusicLayout can\nprovide an additional control signal for steering music generation. This pattern may arise because\nmusic-experienced listeners are more attentive to structural organization when evaluating generated\nmusic. ACE-Step 1.5 has the highest observed fidelity score across the five systems (3.283), above\nGT (3.200). As discussed above, this difference is consistent with the acoustic-quality gap between\nthe MIDI-synthesized data in our experiments and the real recordings used to train ACE-Step 1.5.\nIt underscores the need for matched-data controls to isolate layout planning from training-data\ndifferences.\n12\n\n\fUsing the same dataset scope, we conducted a follow-up on a subset of the previously sampled\nitems. A subset of the experienced participants was shown the GT reference for each item and asked\nto rate the structural similarity of the remaining outputs to the references. The reference-layout\ncondition received a higher observed structural similarity than ACE-Step 1.5, providing subjective\nevidence that MusicLayout captures reference organization and carries it into generated audio. The\ngenerated-layout condition was slightly weaker because coarse dataset prompts do not uniquely\nspecify the GT layout. Once predicted, the layout steers generation toward its own plausible\nstructure. The objective structural metrics above suggest that these outputs can remain structurally\norganized even when they do not reproduce the particular GT structure.\n\n5\n\nConclusion\n\nWe presented MusicLayout, an explicit, time-aligned representation of section organization, development, and instrument arrangement for a unified autoregressive audio LM. Generated before audio\ntokens, it makes structural planning inspectable and adjustable. The same LM plans the layout and\npredicts audio tokens conditioned on it while the synthesis components remain frozen. Our results\nsuggest that MusicLayout provides an interpretable interface that supports long-range structural\norganization and layout-level control.\nDespite these benefits, MusicLayout adjusts structural plans only before synthesis and cannot\nedit existing audio or regenerate selected regions. Our reliance on MIDI-synthesized training audio\nmay also limit audio fidelity. Future work will pursue finer control and higher-quality, structurally\naligned audio data.\n\nAcknowledgments\nThis work was supported by Ant Group.\n\nA\n\nMusicLayout Representation and Annotation\n\nA.1\n\nSerialization Grammar\n\nMusicLayout is serialized as a discrete text sequence with one optional piece-level family block\nand one required segment block. A family records a piece-local identifier, its member segments, its\ncardinality, and whether the members are adjacent. Each segment records an integer-second time\nspan, a functional section label, a texture, an arrangement-change label, its family membership and\nfamily role, the degree of variation from the family prototype, a repetition flag, and a list of active\ninstrument tuples. The grammar used in all reported experiments is summarized below:\n<layout>\n<families>\n<fam> id=fam_K members=sI,sJ count=N adj=true|false </fam>\n</families>\n<segments>\n<seg> id=sI time=A-B label=L texture=T change=C\nfam=fam_K fam_role=R variation=V repeat=true|false\n<roles> instrument:register:density:energy | ... </roles>\n</seg>\n</segments>\n</layout>\n\n13\n\n\fField\n\nAllowed values\n\nSection label\n\nintro, verse, prechorus, chorus, bridge, breakdown, outro, transition, hook,\nsolo, build\nlayered, rhythm_driven, melodic_front, harmonic_bed, percussive,\nbuild_up, sparse_pulse, lead_front, contrast, other\nentry, continuation, lift, drop, contrast, outro\nintro_anchor, primary_repeat, secondary_repeat, adjacent_variant,\noutro_anchor, unique\nunique, same, light_var, strong_var\nlow, mid, high\nsparse, med, dense\nlow, med, high\n\nTexture\nChange\nFamily role\nVariation\nRegister\nDensity\nEnergy\n\nTable 6: Closed structural and arrangement vocabularies in MusicLayout.\nFamily identifiers are local to a piece rather than global semantic labels. For example, fam_0\ndenotes one recurring material within the current piece and may refer to unrelated material in another\npiece. The extraction pipeline retains singleton material as an explicitly declared one-member\nfamily with count=1 and adj=true. Its segment uses variation=unique and repeat=false. For\na recurring family, the first chronological member serves as its variation prototype and is marked\nsame. Later members are marked same, light_var, or strong_var according to their distance from\nthat prototype.\n\nA.2\n\nClosed Vocabularies\n\nTable 6 gives the structural vocabularies. The change field describes the segment’s arrangementlevel relation to its predecessor, whereas fam_role describes its role in the piece-level recurrence\norganization. These fields are distinct from the functional section label.\nThe instrument field uses 25 compact categories: acoustic_piano, electric_piano, plucked_\nkeyboard, mallet_bell, organ, accordion_harmonica, acoustic_guitar, electric_guitar,\nbass, strings, orchestral, synth_strings, choir_voice, brass, sax, woodwind, synth_lead,\nsynth_pad, synth_fx, world_plucked, percussion, reverse_cymbal, sound_fx, drums, and\nother. General MIDI program numbers are mapped deterministically to these categories. Instrument entries retain the segment-local register, density, and energy attributes. These attributes\nare defined at the instrument tuple level rather than duplicated as separate segment-level fields.\n\nA.3\n\nVariation Strength\n\nFor each recurring family, we compare every later segment with the first family member. Let dr be\nthe Jaccard distance between active-role sets, and let de , dd , and da be normalized differences in\nenergy, note density, and active-role count. The base distance is\nd = 0.45dr + 0.20de + 0.20dd + 0.15da .\n\n(4)\n\nThe implementation adds small deterministic adjustments when coarse energy, density, texture,\narrangement-change, repeat-neighbor, or source-relation evidence differs from the family prototype.\nWe serialize scores at most 0.15 as same, scores in (0.15, 0.40] as light_var, and larger scores as\nstrong_var. This field therefore records an interpretable, rule-derived degree of variation rather\nthan a human rating.\n14\n\n\fA.4\n\nValidation\n\nBefore a generated layout can condition audio decoding, the parser checks the outer layout and\nsegment wrappers, unique segment identifiers, nondecreasing integer-second start times, positive\nspans, valid closed-vocabulary values, four-field instrument tuples, family-member counts, and\nreferences from segments to declared families. It also rejects legacy bar spans, legacy role tags,\nsegment-level energy/density fields, internal MIDI program slugs, and audio-code tokens inside the\nlayout. A failed layout is not silently repaired or rewritten.\n\nA.5\n\nAnnotation Pipeline\n\nThe annotation pipeline begins from aligned MIDI and rendered WAV files. It extracts bar-level\nactivity and instrument statistics, combines change and repetition evidence into time-aligned\nsegments, groups structurally related segments into piece-local material families, and derives the\nsection, texture, change, family, variation, and instrument attributes serialized above. MIDI\nprograms are used only as annotation evidence. The learning target remains audio-token generation.\nSegment spans are converted to audio time and rounded to integer seconds, with a minimum\nduration of one second after rounding.\nThe extraction procedure is deterministic once its feature and boundary settings are fixed.\nAlgorithm 1 summarizes its execution order. The symbolic representation supplies the structural\nevidence. The paired waveform is used to place the resulting spans on the rendered-audio timeline.\n\nB\n\nDataset Construction and Provenance\n\nB.1\n\nFreeMIDI\n\nFreeMIDI [12] supplies the training corpus and an in-domain evaluation set. We retained pieces\nlonger than 15 seconds, extracted one MusicLayout from each retained MIDI file, and synthesized\nthe aligned audio at 44.1 kHz using the FluidSynth [11] interface provided by PrettyMIDI [29],\nwith MuseScore_General.sf2 [25]. Of 27,237 extracted items, 27,229 received a full-song caption\nfrom MOSS-Music-8B-Instruct [27]. Caption decoding was greedy. The prompt requested one\nconcise English paragraph grounded only in the audio, covering style, audible instrumentation and\nroles, rhythm, texture, energy, musical development, and mood. It explicitly prohibited the use of\nfilenames, MIDI metadata, external labels, bullet lists, and unsupported claims.\nRequiring at least 15 seconds of audio and a nonempty supervised audio-code span retained\n27,198 items. Five additional items were removed because their complete planning sequences\nexceeded the 4,096-token context. The resulting 27,193 items were split deterministically into\n24,474 training and 2,719 development items with seed 20260501. The development split is also the\nin-domain evaluation set.\n\nB.2\n\nMidiCaps\n\nMidiCaps [24] is an out-of-domain MIDI evaluation set. We first sampled 1,100 items with seed\n42 after requiring a duration of at least 120 seconds, at least two instruments, and a nonempty\ncaption. Genre-quota sampling followed by instrument-diverse greedy selection covered 40 source\ngenre labels. Because the labels are multi-valued, their frequencies need not sum to the number of\nitems. The most frequent labels in the final set are electronic (581), pop (463), rock (208), classical\n(108), soundtrack (102), ambient (81), and jazz (59). Exact SHA-256 comparison of the MIDI files\nagainst the complete FreeMIDI corpus removed 60 overlaps, leaving 1,040 unique items. The source\n15\n\n\fAlgorithm 1 MusicLayout extraction from aligned symbolic music and audio\nRequire: MIDI performance M , aligned waveform duration D, and fixed extraction settings Θ\nEnsure: Valid serialized layout L, or rejection symbol ⊥\n1: function ExtractMusicLayout(M, D, Θ)\n2:\n(N, P, T, B, E) ← ParseSymbolic(M )\n▷ notes, programs, MIDI duration, bars, beats\n3:\nfor b ∈ B do\n4:\nfb ← AggregateBar(N, P, b)\n▷ density, energy, pitch, and six broad-role activities\n5:\nend for\n6:\nq ← AdjacentChangeScores(f )\n7:\nR ← BinarySegmentationCandidates(f, D)\n8:\nq ← AddBoundarySupport(q, R, f )\n9:\nn ← min(14, max(5, Round(D/28)))\n10:\nC ← SelectPeaks(q, mean(q) + 0.34 std(q), 3 bars, n)\n11:\nC ← RefineLongSpans(C, f, B)\n▷ adjacent-repeat and local-change tests\n12:\nC ← InsertMicroBoundaries(C, f, B)\n13:\nS ← ScaleBoundariesToAudio(C, B, T, D)\n14:\nS ← MergeShortSegments(S, 8 s)\n15:\nS ← RefineOpeningWithBeats(S, E, f, B, T, D)\n16:\nfor si ∈ S do\n17:\nzi ← AggregateSegment({fb : b ⊆ si })\n18:\nei ← AggregateInstrumentStatistics(N, P, si )\n19:\nend for\n20:\nF ←∅\n21:\nfor i = 1, . . . , |S| do\n▷ chronological greedy centroid assignment\n22:\nk∗ ← arg maxk cos(zi , µk )\n23:\nif F = ∅ or cos(zi , µk∗ ) < 0.965 then\n24:\nϕ(i) ← NewFamily(F, zi )\n25:\nelse\n26:\nϕ(i) ← k∗ , µk∗ ← MemberMean({zj : ϕ(j) = k∗ })\n27:\nend if\n28:\nend for\n29:\nfor i, j ∈ {1, . . . , |S|} do\n30:\nAij ← cos(zi , zj )\n▷ relation evidence, not family assignment\n31:\nend for\n|S|\n32:\n(ℓi , ti , ci , ui )i=1 ← AssignDescriptors(S, z, A, F, ϕ)\n33:\nfor Fk ∈ F do\n34:\npk ← min{i : ϕ(i) = k}\n35:\nfor i : ϕ(i) = k do\n36:\nvi ← VariationClass(si , spk , Θvariation )\n37:\nend for\n38:\nend for\n39:\nK ← MapProgramsToCategories(P )\n40:\nfor si ∈ S do\n41:\nri ← QuantizeInstrumentTuples(ei , K)\n42:\nend for\n|S|\n43:\nL ← SerializeAndRound(F, {si , ℓi , ti , ci , ui , vi , ri }i=1 )\n44:\nif ¬Validate(L) then\n45:\nreturn ⊥\n46:\nend if\n47:\nreturn L\n48: end function\n\nlocation and MIDI SHA-256 jointly define item identity. We then extracted MusicLayouts and\nsynthesized aligned audio using the same renderer as for FreeMIDI. Original MidiCaps captions\nwere retained as prompts, and no MidiCaps item was used for training.\n\nB.3\n\nMuChin\n\nMuChin [38] provides an out-of-domain real-audio evaluation. Because it does not provide MIDI, it\ncannot supply a reference MusicLayout. We constructed a fixed 1,000-item manifest in the source\nmetadata order. For each item, DeepSeek-V4-Flash [40] received the original full-song description\nas context and a set of non-vocal tags covering instrumentation, arrangement, style, tempo, rhythm,\nand mood. The instruction required one concise English paragraph using only these safe musical\n16\n\n\fSetting\n\nStage 1\n\nStage 2\n\nTasks\nEpoch limit\nLearning rate\nMonitored development\ntask\nEarly-stopping patience\nTrainable scope\nLoss-bearing targets\n\nLayout planning\n10", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 1000, "totalLines": 2074}}}
