{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b072bbb50a241963", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1500\t1.0 × 10−4\n1501\tPlanning\n1502\t\n1503\tLayout planning and layout-to-audio\n1504\t100\n1505\t3.0 × 10−5\n1506\tLayout-to-audio\n1507\t\n1508\t5\n1509\tNew embedding/LM-head rows\n1510\tLayout span\n1511\t\n1512\t10\n1513\tLM\n1514\tLayout or audio span for the sampled\n1515\ttask\n1516\t\n1517\tTable 8: Stage-specific optimization protocol. The best Stage-2 checkpoint is restored according to\n1518\tdevelopment layout-to-audio loss.\n1519\tattributes and explicitly prohibited any mention or implication of singers, vocals, lyrics, rap, choir,\n1520\thumming, or spoken dialogue. Decoding used temperature 0.2 and top-p 0.9. We appended the\n1521\tsame explicit instrumental-only control phrase to every final prompt.\n1522\tWe separated accompaniment from vocals with the Python toolkit audio-separator v0.44.3 [3]\n1523\tand the BS-RoFormer [22] Viperx-1297 checkpoint [35], distributed as model_bs_roformer_ep_317_\n1524\tsdr_12.9755.ckpt. We used the resulting accompaniment as the metric reference. Reference-layout\n1525\tand inference-time shuffled-reference-layout conditions are consequently not applicable to MuChin.\n1526\tDataset\n1527\t\n1528\tTrain Evaluation Ref. layout\n1529\t\n1530\tFreeMIDI 24,474\n1531\tMidiCaps\n1532\t0\n1533\tMuChin\n1534\t0\n1535\t\n1536\t2,719\n1537\t1,040\n1538\t1,000\n1539\t\n1540\tYes\n1541\tYes\n1542\tNo\n1543\t\n1544\tTable 7: Dataset roles and final item counts.\n1545\t\n1546\tC\n1547\t\n1548\tModel Adaptation and Training\n1549\t\n1550\tC.1\n1551\t\n1552\tTraining Sequences and Loss Masks\n1553\t\n1554\tThe adapted model uses the 1.7B-parameter LM from ACE-Step 1.5 [13]. Layout planning and\n1555\taudio-token prediction share the same LM but use separate supervised target spans. In the planning\n1556\ttask, the prompt and chat context are inputs and the MusicLayout tokens are targets. In the\n1557\tlayout-to-audio task, the prompt and ground-truth MusicLayout form the input prefix and only the\n1558\tfollowing audio-code tokens are targets. Chain-of-thought metadata is retained in the serialized\n1559\tprefix but receives zero loss weight. The pretrained 5-Hz audio tokenizer and DiT renderer [28]\n1560\tremain frozen in all stages.\n1561\tStage 1 trains only the newly introduced vocabulary rows in the input embedding and LM head.\n1562\tOld vocabulary rows are masked, and no transformer layer or final normalization parameter is\n1563\tupdated. Stage 2 updates the LM while alternating the planning and layout-to-audio tasks. The\n1564\tdevelopment layout-to-audio loss determines checkpoint selection.\n1565\tTraining used six 80-GB GPUs, a maximum sequence length of 4,096, per-rank batch size\n1566\t2, gradient accumulation 4, and effective batch size 48. Parameters and AdamW [21] optimizer\n1567\tstates remained FP32, while FSDP [41] computation used BF16. Full-shard FSDP used original\n1568\tparameters, automatic wrapping, synchronized module states, limited all-gathers, and replicated\n1569\t17\n1570\t\n1571\t\fvocabulary parameters for the Stage-1 row masks. The warmup ratio was 0.05 and the training\n1572\trandom seed was 42. The data split used seed 20260501.\n1573\t\n1574\tC.2\n1575\t\n1576\tMatched-Data Controls\n1577\t\n1578\tThe no-layout ACE-Step 1.5-FT control uses the same filtered training items, audio codes, backbone\n1579\tLM, optimization precision, and adaptation budget as the full model, but directly predicts audio\n1580\ttokens without a MusicLayout prefix. The shuffled-layout-training control retains the MusicLayoutshaped prefix but pairs each audio target with a layout from another item during training. At\n1581\tinference it generates a layout from the prompt and then generates audio, as in the full generatedlayout condition. The shuffled-layout-inference control uses the normally trained MusicLayout\n1582\tmodel but replaces the item-matched reference layout with a valid layout from another item. Thus,\n1583\tthe three controls remove layout information, disrupt training-time correspondence, or disrupt\n1584\tinference-time correspondence, respectively.\n1585\tBoth shuffled conditions use seed 20260711. For shuffled-layout training, the 24,474 training\n1586\tlayouts form a one-to-one permutation of the same training items, with zero fixed points. The\n1587\t2,719 development items remain unchanged. For shuffled-layout inference, FreeMIDI and MidiCaps\n1588\tare permuted independently within their evaluation sets. This yields 3,759 unique target–donor\n1589\tpairs and zero fixed points, preventing cross-dataset donors and self-matches. Donor layouts are\n1590\ttruncated or extended at segment boundaries to end at the target item’s requested duration.\n1591\t\n1592\tD\n1593\t\n1594\tBaseline Selection and Exact Configurations\n1595\t\n1596\tD.1\n1597\t\n1598\tSelection Scope\n1599\t\n1600\tWe restricted the baseline comparison to models with publicly released weights and executable\n1601\tinference implementations. We further required compatibility with instrumental prompt-to-music\n1602\tgeneration and several-minute output, either through native variable-duration synthesis or through\n1603\tan established continuation mechanism. These criteria allow the reported systems to consume the\n1604\tsame prompts and target durations and to be evaluated using the same audio and structural metrics.\n1605\tAudioLDM 2 [20] and Mustango [23] were considered but not included in the final comparison.\n1606\tTheir released checkpoints and public inference protocols are centered on short clips and do not\n1607\tprovide a validated overlapping-context continuation procedure for the several-minute setting used\n1608\there. Concatenating independently generated diffusion samples would introduce a different and\n1609\tpotentially discontinuous generation procedure. In contrast, the reported diffusion baseline, Stable\n1610\tAudio 3 Medium [10], supports the required duration natively.\n1611\t\n1612\tD.2\n1613\t\n1614\tExecuted Configurations\n1615\t\n1616\tTable 9 reports the configurations used in the final comparison. Each system targets the item’s\n1617\tmanifest duration. ACE-Step-family and Stable Audio systems generate that duration natively,\n1618\twithout concatenating independently generated clips. The corresponding model families are described\n1619\tby Gong et al. [13] and Evans et al. [10].\n1620\tBoth ACE-Step configurations use the released LM-assisted inference path: the LM first generates\n1621\tchain-of-thought metadata and audio semantic codes, which are subsequently rendered by the DiT.\n1622\tWe therefore treat ACE-Step 1.5 and ACE-Step 1.5 XL-Turbo as hybrid LM–DiT baselines rather\n1623\tthan pure diffusion baselines. Stable Audio 3 Medium is the diffusion-only baseline in this comparison.\n1624\t\n1625\t18\n1626\t\n1627\t\fSystem\n1628\t\n1629\tCheckpoint/configuration\n1630\t\n1631\tExecuted inference settings\n1632\t\n1633\tMusicGen-Large\n1634\t\n1635\tfacebook/musicgen-large\n1636\t\n1637\tACE-Step 1.5\n1638\t\n1639\tacestep-v15-turbo,\n1640\tacestep-5Hz-lm-1.7B\n1641\t\n1642\t30-s window, 10-s audio context, 20-s retained\n1643\thop, 51.2 tokens/s, 1,536 first-window and\n1644\t1,024 continuation tokens, base seed 28602\n1645\t8 inference steps, LM temperature/top-p\n1646\t0.9/0.95, native target duration, base seed\n1647\t38602\n1648\t8 inference steps,\n1649\tshift 3.0,\n1650\tLM\n1651\ttemperature/top-p 0.9/0.95, native target duration, base seed 38602\n1652\t8 sampling steps, CFG 1.0, no negative\n1653\tprompt, FP16, chunked decoding, native target duration, base seed 48602\n1654\t\n1655\tACE-Step 1.5 XL- acestep-v15-xl-turbo,\n1656\tTurbo\n1657\tacestep-5Hz-lm-1.7B\n1658\tStable Audio\n1659\tMedium\n1660\t\n1661\t3 stable-audio-3-medium\n1662\t\n1663\tTable 9: Exact configurations of the externally pretrained baselines. Base seeds are combined with\n1664\tthe global item index.\n1665\tFor MusicGen-Large [4], the first window is generated from the text prompt alone. Each later\n1666\twindow receives the same text and the final 10 seconds of the accumulated waveform as audio\n1667\tcontext, generates at most 1,024 new codec tokens, and retains at most 20 seconds of new audio.\n1668\tThe window seed is the item seed plus the zero-based window index. The concatenated result is\n1669\tcropped only at the final target duration.\n1670\t\n1671\tE\n1672\t\n1673\tInference and Evaluation Protocol\n1674\t\n1675\tE.1\n1676\t\n1677\tMusicLayout Inference\n1678\t\n1679\tAutomatic inference first decodes the chain-of-thought metadata and MusicLayout, validates the\n1680\tcomplete layout, and then continues with 5-Hz audio-code tokens. Only the outer special-token\n1681\tboundaries are constrained. the decoded metadata and layout body are not inserted, replaced,\n1682\tor rewritten. Planning uses temperature/top-p 0.8/0.95, while audio-code decoding uses 0.9/0.95.\n1683\tIn the generated-layout condition, the final endpoint of the validated layout determines both the\n1684\taudio-code target count and renderer duration. A layout ending at D seconds therefore requests\n1685\t5D audio-code tokens. Reference- and shuffled-layout conditions likewise use the endpoint of the\n1686\tsupplied effective layout. Systems without a layout representation target the reference manifest\n1687\tduration.\n1688\t\n1689\tE.2\n1690\t\n1691\tAudio Standardization and Pairing\n1692\t\n1693\tEvery metric consumes a non-destructively normalized copy of each waveform. Audio is converted to\n1694\tmono, 44.1-kHz PCM16 and normalized with two-pass EBU R128 [8] to −14 LUFS with a −1 dBTP\n1695\ttrue-peak ceiling. FAD and CLAPScore use the complete normalized generated clip. PaSST-KL,\n1696\tSSIM, and acoustic-boundary agreement operate on filename-matched generated/reference pairs\n1697\tcropped from time zero to their exact common-minimum duration. No waveform is time-stretched,\n1698\tlooped, or zero-filled to imitate missing musical content.\n1699\t\n1700\t19\n1701\t\n1702\t\fE.3\n1703\t\n1704\tMetric Implementations\n1705\t\n1706\tFAD [30] compares VGGish [15] embedding distributions. PaSST-KL [17] uses non-overlapping\n1707\t10-second windows and averages KL(pref ∥pgen ) across aligned windows. SSIM [37] is computed\n1708\tover paired mel spectrograms. CLAPScore [7] averages prompt–audio cosine similarity over nonoverlapping 10-second windows, zero-padding only the last partial window.\n1709\tFor structural complexity, each clip is represented by the 26-dimensional SCM descriptor\n1710\tvector [5]. Within a dataset, the reference and generated vectors are standardized before computing\n1711\tthe empirical Energy Distance [33]:\n1712\tn X\n1713\tm\n1714\t2 X\n1715\tE(R, G) =\n1716\t∥ri − gj ∥2\n1717\tnm i=1 j=1\n1718\tn\n1719\t1 X\n1720\t∥ri − ri′ ∥2\n1721\t− 2\n1722\tn i,i′ =1\n1723\tm\n1724\t1 X\n1725\t− 2\n1726\t∥gj − gj ′ ∥2 .\n1727\tm j,j ′ =1\n1728\t\n1729\t(5)\n1730\t\n1731\tSCM Energy Distance measures agreement between corpus-level structural complexity distributions.\n1732\tIt is not an item-level measure of whether a system followed the correct layout.\n1733\tAcoustic-boundary agreement first extracts time–frequency features, clusters frames with fixed\n1734\tk = 6, and obtains a boundary sequence for both reference and generated audio. The reported\n1735\tF0.5 and F3.0 use 0.5- and 3-second tolerances with the standard segment-boundary evaluation\n1736\tprotocol [36]. Unlike SCM, these scores compare transitions within paired items, although they do\n1737\tnot identify which MusicLayout field caused a boundary.\n1738\t\n1739\tF\n1740\t\n1741\tDescriptive Plan and Output Audits\n1742\t\n1743\tThe main results measure the realized audio rather than treating a textual layout as correct merely\n1744\tbecause it parses. Before audio decoding, we parsed and schema-validated every sampled layout.\n1745\tAn invalid sample was discarded and sampling was repeated within a finite retry budget, stopping\n1746\tas soon as a valid layout was obtained. This procedure produced a valid MusicLayout for every\n1747\trequested evaluation item: all 2,719 FreeMIDI, 1,040 MidiCaps, and 1,000 MuChin items were\n1748\tsuccessfully validated and rendered. Thus, no evaluation item was removed because of layout\n1749\tinvalidity or rendering failure. Table 10 summarizes the resulting layout lengths. these descriptive\n1750\tchecks are not substitutes for audio evaluation.\n1751\t\n1752\tG\n1753\t\n1754\tStructural Evaluation and Layout Manipulation\n1755\t\n1756\tG.1\n1757\t\n1758\tRecurrence Visualization\n1759\t\n1760\tThe self-similarity matrices in the paper are recurrence visualizations rather than scalar modelranking metrics. The analysis uses 12-bin harmonic pitch class profiles, a 209-ms analysis window,\n1761\ta 139-ms hop, delay-coordinate embedding over approximately three seconds, and a mutual 4%\n1762\tnearest-neighbor recurrence rule following the structure-analysis pipeline of Serra et al. [32]. Within\n1763\teach target–donor case, all signals are cropped from time zero to their common minimum duration\n1764\tbefore SSM computation. Display intensity is used to make recurrence patterns legible. It does not\n1765\tenter any quantitative score.\n1766\t20\n1767\t\n1768\t\fFreeMIDI FreeMIDI MidiCaps MuChin\n1769\ttrain\n1770\tgenerated generated generated\n1771\t\n1772\tStatistic\n1773\tItems\n1774\tSegments\n1775\tFamilies\n1776\tRecurring families\n1777\tEndpoint (s)\n1778\t\n1779\t24,474\n1780\t11.31\n1781\t2.28\n1782\t1.63\n1783\t225.34\n1784\t\n1785\t2,719\n1786\t9.73\n1787\t1.67\n1788\t1.37\n1789\t187.44\n1790\t\n1791\t1,040\n1792\t8.38\n1793\t1.50\n1794\t1.28\n1795\t163.36\n1796\t\n1797\t1,000\n1798\t8.66\n1799\t1.50\n1800\t1.21\n1801\t170.01\n1802\t\n1803\tTable 10: The FreeMIDI train column reports statistics of the MusicLayouts obtained by applying\n1804\tAlgorithm 1 to the FreeMIDI training split. The other three columns summarize valid MusicLayouts\n1805\tgenerated for evaluation. Except for item counts, entries are per-item means. A recurring family\n1806\tcontains at least two segments. Endpoint is the final serialized segment boundary.\n1807\t\n1808\tG.2\n1809\t\n1810\tCross-System Recurrence Examples\n1811\t\n1812\tFigure 5 expands the recurrence visualization to the systems in the main objective comparison.\n1813\tIt shows two cases from each of the three evaluation datasets (six rows in total). Each generated\n1814\texample has the same duration as its corresponding ground-truth audio. Within each row, all\n1815\tavailable signals are cropped from time zero to the common minimum duration before recurrence\n1816\tanalysis. The reference-layout and shuffled-layout conditions are unavailable for MuChin and are\n1817\tmarked N/A.\n1818\tMusicGen-Large primarily exhibits near-diagonal or locally repeated patterns and less consistently\n1819\trecovers the distant off-diagonal organization visible in the ground truth. This behavior is consistent\n1820\twith its overlapping-context generation protocol: a later window receives only the retained tail\n1821\tof the preceding audio, so patterns from much earlier windows are no longer directly available as\n1822\tcontext. Its local continuity therefore does not by itself preserve long-range recurrence.\n1823\tOn the MIDI-derived rows, the reference-layout condition most closely preserves the salient block\n1824\tand stripe organization of the corresponding ground-truth SSMs. This provides qualitative evidence\n1825\tthat MusicLayout captures musically relevant recurrence structure and that the layout-conditioned\n1826\trenderer can realize that structure in audio. Across the six cases, the generated-layout condition\n1827\tis less closely aligned with the specific reference, as expected when the layout is predicted from\n1828\ttext rather than extracted from the target piece, but it still shows repeated blocks and off-diagonal\n1829\tpatterns spanning the generated piece. These examples therefore support reasonable autonomous\n1830\tlong-range organization without treating the SSM visualization as a scalar model ranking.\n1831\t\n1832\tG.3\n1833\t\n1834\tCropping Details for Layout Manipulation\n1835\t\n1836\tTable 11 reports the crop durations used for the four rows of the layout-manipulation SSM in\n1837\tFigure 3. For each row, the duration is the minimum available duration among the four signals\n1838\tshown in that row. All signals are cropped from time zero without temporal alignment, time\n1839\tstretching, or padding. This operation only establishes a shared visualization interval. It does not\n1840\tmodify the underlying recurrence computation or enter the objective metrics.\n1841\tCase\n1842\t\n1843\tCommon duration (s)\n1844\t\n1845\tFreeMIDI 1\n1846\tFreeMIDI 2\n1847\tMidiCaps 1\n1848\tMidiCaps 2\n1849\t\n1850\t224.4\n1851\t194.6\n1852\t137.4\n1853\t179.716\n1854\t\n1855\tTable 11: Common-minimum crop durations for the layout-manipulation SSM rows in Figure 3.\n1856\t\n1857\t21\n1858\t\n1859\t\fGT\n1860\t\n1861\tACE-1.5\n1862\t\n1863\tACE-XL\n1864\t\n1865\tMusicGen-L\n1866\t\n1867\tSA3-M\n1868\t\n1869\tOurs-Ref\n1870\t\n1871\tOurs-Gen\n1872\t\n1873\tACE-FT\n1874\t\n1875\tOurs-Shuf\n1876\t\n1877\tFreeMIDI A\n1878\t\n1879\tFreeMIDI B\n1880\t\n1881\tMidiCaps A\n1882\t\n1883\tMidiCaps B\n1884\t\n1885\tMuChin A\n1886\t\n1887\tN/A\n1888\t\n1889\tN/A\n1890\t\n1891\tMuChin B\n1892\t\n1893\tN/A\n1894\t\n1895\tN/A\n1896\t\n1897\tFigure 5: Cross-system recurrence SSMs for six cases, with two cases each from FreeMIDI, MidiCaps,\n1898\tand MuChin. Darker cells indicate stronger recurrence within a panel. Following the visualization\n1899\tin the main article, intensities are normalized independently within each panel and therefore should\n1900\tnot be compared as absolute recurrence density across systems. Every row is cropped to a common\n1901\tduration. Each cell summarizes the mean density of a 16 × 16 block of the underlying binary\n1902\trecurrence matrix, corresponding to approximately 2.23 seconds. A monotonic power-law display\n1903\ttransform (γ = 0.45) improves the visibility of sparse recurrence patterns without altering the\n1904\trecurrence matrices. N/A denotes a condition not available for the MuChin evaluation.\n1905\t\n1906\tH\n1907\t\n1908\tDiscussion\n1909\t\n1910\tBeyond explicit musical planning, MusicLayout shows how structured representations can amplify\n1911\tthe value of incremental data for music audio generation. MIDI expands the data available to an\n1912\taudio model, while extracting layouts further unlocks its temporal and arrangement information.\n1913\tThe resulting prompt–layout–audio triples turn the same incremental corpus into richer supervision,\n1914\timproving generation while enabling structure-aware planning and control. Thus, the value of\n1915\tadditional data depends not only on its scale, but also on how its latent structure is represented\n1916\tand exploited.\n1917\t\n1918\t22\n1919\t\n1920\t\fI\n1921\t\n1922\tQualitative Scope and Limitations\n1923\t\n1924\tMusicLayout is a pre-synthesis planning interface. It can change the prefix that conditions a new\n1925\twaveform, but it does not directly edit an existing waveform or regenerate a selected region while\n1926\tpreserving all other samples. The categorical instrument representation describes arrangement-level\n1927\tsource classes rather than exact timbres, performances, or production effects. A valid layout is\n1928\ttherefore a well-formed structural request, not a guarantee that every specified attribute will be\n1929\trealized perfectly in audio.\n1930\tThe model is trained on MIDI-synthesized instrumental audio. This provides aligned symbolic\n1931\tstructure at scale but limits acoustic diversity and fidelity relative to models trained primarily\n1932\ton studio recordings. It also creates a domain shift for MuChin, whose evaluation references are\n1933\taccompaniments separated from real vocal recordings. Consequently, a syntactically valid layout\n1934\tmay still yield audio in which a requested transition, recurrence, or instrument entry is weak or\n1935\tunclear.\n1936\t\n1937\tReferences\n1938\t[1] Andrea Agostinelli, Timo I Denk, Zalán Borsos, Jesse Engel, Mauro Verzetti, Antoine Caillon,\n1939\tQingqing Huang, Aren Jansen, Adam Roberts, Marco Tagliasacchi, et al. Musiclm: Generating\n1940\tmusic from text. arXiv preprint arXiv:2301.11325, 2023.\n1941\t[2] Ye Bai, Haonan Chen, Jitong Chen, Zhuo Chen, Yi Deng, Xiaohong Dong, Lamtharn Hantrakul,\n1942\tWeituo Hao, Qingqing Huang, Zhongyi Huang, et al. Seed-music: A unified framework for high\n1943\tquality and controlled music generation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2409.09214, 2024.\n1944\t[3] Andrew Beveridge. Audio Separator, version 0.44.3. https://github.com/nomadkaraoke/\n1945\tpython-audio-separator/releases/tag/v0.44.3, 2026. Accessed July 30, 2026.\n1946\t[4] Jade Copet, Felix Kreuk, Itai Gat, Tal Remez, David Kant, Gabriel Synnaeve, Yossi Adi,\n1947\tand Alexandre Défossez. Simple and controllable music generation. In Advances in Neural\n1948\tInformation Processing Systems, volume 36, 2023.\n1949\t[5] Jacopo De Berardinis, Angelo Cangelosi, and Eduardo Coutinho. Measuring the structural\n1950\tcomplexity of music: From structural segmentations to the automatic evaluation of models for\n1951\tmusic generation. IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, 30:\n1952\t1963–1976, 2022. doi: 10.1109/TASLP.2022.3178203.\n1953\t[6] Prafulla Dhariwal, Heewoo Jun, Christine Payne, Jong Wook Kim, Alec Radford, and Ilya\n1954\tSutskever. Jukebox: A generative model for music. arXiv preprint arXiv:2005.00341, 2020.\n1955\t[7] Benjamin Elizalde, Soham Deshmukh, Mahmoud Al Ismail, and Huaming Wang. CLAP:\n1956\tLearning audio concepts from natural language supervision. In 2023 I